WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.437
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4545.

2
00:00:01.437 --> 00:00:02.874
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:02.874 --> 00:00:03.353
<v JENNIFER ABRAHAM CRAMER>Alright.

4
00:00:03.353 --> 00:00:07.185
So today is November sixth the year 2015,

5
00:00:07.185 --> 00:00:20.597
and this is Jennifer Abraham Cramer with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries in partnership with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum.

6
00:00:20.597 --> 00:00:28.261
And today we're here with Butch Lemelle . . . Edward Butch Lemelle, and this is interview number two.

7
00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:30.176
We're session number two.

8
00:00:30.176 --> 00:00:39.756
The first interview that we did was at oral history day, so it was about half an hour or so.

9
00:00:39.756 --> 00:00:48.857
And we're here today to do a longer interview and to pick up kind of where we left off.

10
00:00:48.857 --> 00:00:52.210
So . . . [phone rings] we'll let that ring.

11
00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:53.647
That's no problem.

12
00:00:53.647 --> 00:00:57.000
Is that your phone right there?

13
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.556
<v EDWARD BUTCH LEMELLE JR>I was supposed to turn it off, and I didn't.

14
00:01:00.556 --> 00:01:02.333
I’m cutting my camera off.

15
00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:06.956
Y'all just say excuse me on the interview, I'm cutting my phone off.

16
00:01:06.956 --> 00:01:11.933
I never cut it off because I never know if I'm having an emergency.

17
00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:12.289
<v CRAMER>Yeah.

18
00:01:12.289 --> 00:01:15.489
<v LEMELLE>Or my pastor's wife, but I'll call her back.

19
00:01:15.489 --> 00:01:16.200
<v CRAMER>Oh okay.

20
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.978
I mean, you can take . . .

21
00:01:17.978 --> 00:01:19.400
<v LEMELLE>Oh no, I'm good.

22
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.178
I'm just cutting it off.

23
00:01:21.178 --> 00:01:22.600
Cutting it down low.

24
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:23.667
That's on camera?

25
00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:24.378
<v CRAMER>It's okay.

26
00:01:24.378 --> 00:01:25.089
It's a . . .

27
00:01:25.089 --> 00:01:27.222
<v LEMELLE>You’re going to cut that out.

28
00:01:27.222 --> 00:01:27.578
<v CRAMER>Yeah.

29
00:01:27.578 --> 00:01:36.467
So we got a pretty good family tree last time from you with your father's family, and your mother's family, and that sort of thing.

30
00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:47.844
And there were a couple of things that I wanted to see if I could get some additional information on in regards to your grandfather on your mother's side and also . . . Okay,

31
00:01:47.844 --> 00:01:53.889
so your grandfather on your mother's side you said he was the son of a former slave,

32
00:01:53.889 --> 00:02:01.000
and that . . . you said he took the name of the former slave owner, but we didn't catch his name.

33
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:01.589
<v LEMELLE>T-O-W-N-E-R.

34
00:02:01.589 --> 00:02:02.178
<v CRAMER>Okay.

35
00:02:02.178 --> 00:02:11.012
<v LEMELLE>His father, my great grandfather, the father of David Duff Towner, his name was Israel.

36
00:02:11.012 --> 00:02:15.723
And somewhere . . . I'm trying to get the name.

37
00:02:15.723 --> 00:02:26.913
I know my grandfather's . . . My great grandfather was Israel Towner, but prior to that there was another family name.

38
00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:37.514
But from what we gather, that Israel, my great grandfather, taken the Towner name from a slave owner.

39
00:02:37.514 --> 00:02:41.047
And that went down through generations.

40
00:02:41.047 --> 00:02:51.059
Later on my grandfather and his sisters and brothers found out through history that they were Ilses.

41
00:02:51.059 --> 00:02:56.360
Their name was Ilses before . . . One of the great-great-

42
00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.893
<c.p>great grandfathers, he was an Ilses.

43
00:02:59.893 --> 00:03:08.727
When you get with Ms. Lenoria, she has a lot of history because we're cousins.

44
00:03:08.727 --> 00:03:21.095
And this is the understanding, that I gather, that it was the Ilses family and that name was changed to Towner.

45
00:03:21.095 --> 00:03:29.340
So when . . . Later on, this is why up around DeRidder, [Louisiana] part of DeRidder,

46
00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:41.119
there was some people we met that were Ilses and found out that we were relatives through the Ilses family.

47
00:03:41.119 --> 00:03:43.474
And he's a minister.

48
00:03:43.474 --> 00:03:49.364
So this is the part that I was talking about.

49
00:03:49.364 --> 00:03:53.486
My grandfather, his father, was a slave.

50
00:03:53.486 --> 00:04:03.498
And that made my great-great grandfather a slave and taken the name Towner from what I understand.

51
00:04:03.498 --> 00:04:09.387
<v LEONA LEMELLE>So actually, y’all don't have no Towner blood in there.

52
00:04:09.387 --> 00:04:09.976
<v LEMELLE>Yes.

53
00:04:09.976 --> 00:04:11.154
<v LEONA LEMELLE>The Ilseses.

54
00:04:11.154 --> 00:04:15.277
<v LEMELLE>The only thing is the name changed.

55
00:04:15.277 --> 00:04:26.466
We still have my grandfather's blood, and we go by Towner because that's what he was called, a Towner.

56
00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:30.000
<v CRAMER>How do you spell Ilses?

57
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.100
<v LEMELLE>I-L-S-E-S I think.

58
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:33.500
I-L-S-E-S, Ilses.

59
00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:35.600
<v CRAMER>Is that French?

60
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.700
<v LEONA LEMELLE>It's not French.

61
00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.800
<v CRAMER>What is it?

62
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:41.200
<v LEMELLE>I think . . .

63
00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.300
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I don't know.

64
00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:44.700
<v CRAMER>Spanish, maybe?

65
00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:49.600
<v LEMELLE>I think Ilses is more of a . . .

66
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Italian?

67
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.315
<v LEMELLE>No. Whenever . . . Just make you a note.

68
00:04:55.315 --> 00:05:03.329
When you're talking to Lenoria Ambrose she has a lot of history, too.

69
00:05:03.329 --> 00:05:03.945
<v CRAMER>Okay.

70
00:05:03.945 --> 00:05:08.260
<v LEMELLE>And we tie all this in together.

71
00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:08.877
<v CRAMER>Okay.

72
00:05:08.877 --> 00:05:10.726
That's very interesting.

73
00:05:10.726 --> 00:05:16.890
<v LEMELLE>Now my grandfather's wife, my grandmother, she was a Pots.

74
00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:26.753
And we did a little bit of talking about my grandmother with her beautiful green eyes.

75
00:05:26.753 --> 00:05:30.452
She was a Pots by birth.

76
00:05:30.452 --> 00:05:34.151
And her mother was a slave.

77
00:05:34.151 --> 00:05:36.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Grandma's mother?

78
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.349
<v LEMELLE>My grandmother's . . . My grandmother Pots, her mother was a slave.

79
00:05:41.349 --> 00:05:56.326
We did a little touch-up on it because my wife remembered I had an aunty named [Etter?] that my wife know and love that you dare ask questions . . .

80
00:05:56.326 --> 00:05:56.860
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

81
00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:07.023
<v LEMELLE>. . . about my grandmother, of why she was so fair and why she had green eyes and all these things.

82
00:06:07.023 --> 00:06:09.163
As kids we wondered.

83
00:06:09.163 --> 00:06:19.860
But being a slave, her mother knew you had to do, what you did, what they said do or whatever.

84
00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.140
And, you know, I’m telling you what happened.

85
00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:42.326
Because if you'd seen pictures of my grandmother's mother and her brother who was . . . his name was [Elic?] Pots and he was really a dark skinned man taken from his mother and his father.

86
00:06:42.326 --> 00:06:48.744
But my grandmother had more the genes of her biological slave-owning father.

87
00:06:48.744 --> 00:06:50.884
Put it that way.

88
00:06:50.884 --> 00:06:57.302
But we dare talked about that because it was more of a . . .

89
00:06:57.302 --> 00:06:59.442
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I'd say a secret.

90
00:06:59.442 --> 00:07:01.581
It was [holding back?].

91
00:07:01.581 --> 00:07:06.395
<v LEMELLE>It wasn't a secret, but it was something shameful.

92
00:07:06.395 --> 00:07:08.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah, shameful.

93
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:17.434
<v LEMELLE>More shameful, because my grandmother always looked at it being a terrible thing to have happened to her mother.

94
00:07:17.434 --> 00:07:26.372
And I did tell you about my grandfather, and I think we . . . if I can remember correctly too,

95
00:07:26.372 --> 00:07:33.324
I said more self-employed, but he did do work besides what I told you.

96
00:07:33.324 --> 00:07:42.759
He did go out and do private work in private homes . . . private maintenance in . . . Just called it private duty.

97
00:07:42.759 --> 00:07:50.207
He did homes and yard work, whatever was necessary to do to make a living.

98
00:07:50.207 --> 00:07:55.669
But most of his was doing for himself and his family.

99
00:07:55.669 --> 00:07:58.648
<v CRAMER>You said he had carpentry skills . . .

100
00:07:58.648 --> 00:07:59.145
<v LEMELLE>No.

101
00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:00.634
<v CRAMER>. . . and sold coal?

102
00:08:00.634 --> 00:08:03.614
No, your grandmother had carpentry skills.

103
00:08:03.614 --> 00:08:10.566
<v LEMELLE>He had the skills of . . . He was more like one that . . . He made coals,

104
00:08:10.566 --> 00:08:14.041
but my grandmother had the carpenter skills.

105
00:08:14.041 --> 00:08:14.538
Grandmother.

106
00:08:14.538 --> 00:08:17.021
<v CRAMER>And he sold the coal.

107
00:08:17.021 --> 00:08:20.000
He did the coal selling.

108
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:24.417
<v LEMELLE>He made coal from burning of different type wood.

109
00:08:24.417 --> 00:08:28.833
And he . . . Once it was cold, he'd cool it.

110
00:08:28.833 --> 00:08:30.306
He sold coal.

111
00:08:30.306 --> 00:08:32.759
<v CRAMER>He was the gardener, right?

112
00:08:32.759 --> 00:08:34.722
<v LEMELLE>He was the gardener.

113
00:08:34.722 --> 00:08:36.194
He could grow.

114
00:08:36.194 --> 00:08:38.648
They used to tease him.

115
00:08:38.648 --> 00:08:44.537
They said if he planted four tires he would grow an automobile.

116
00:08:44.537 --> 00:08:47.972
He taught my father a lot, too.

117
00:08:47.972 --> 00:09:00.731
A lot of those things that when my father came into the family, my grandfather taught him a lot of things in gardening and raising children.

118
00:09:00.731 --> 00:09:04.167
<v CRAMER>So how long did your grandfather live?

119
00:09:04.167 --> 00:09:08.093
How old did he live to be about?

120
00:09:08.093 --> 00:09:12.019
<v LEMELLE>My grandfather lived to be in his eighties.

121
00:09:12.019 --> 00:09:13.000
<v CRAMER>Okay.

122
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:13.497
<v LEMELLE>Yes.

123
00:09:13.497 --> 00:09:16.479
Well, somewhere in his early eighties.

124
00:09:16.479 --> 00:09:25.923
The reason I say early eighties is because I was six years old when they had their fifty-year anniversary.

125
00:09:25.923 --> 00:09:31.391
So I'm saying if he was like in his twenties . . . fifty,

126
00:09:31.391 --> 00:09:37.852
and he lived like at least ten year prior to their fifty-year anniversary.

127
00:09:37.852 --> 00:09:44.314
So I'm giving him somewhere . . . I think my grandfather . . . It's all out there.

128
00:09:44.314 --> 00:09:53.260
I had it on my camera because I’d go out and take pictures of the graves and stuff.

129
00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:59.225
And all that's on that . . . We went to Mount Zion [Baptist Church].

130
00:09:59.225 --> 00:10:04.692
All that's on the headstones is his birthdate and his death.

131
00:10:04.692 --> 00:10:08.669
But I think he was in his eighties.

132
00:10:08.669 --> 00:10:19.107
<v CRAMER>So is there anything else that you want to add about your family that came before you before we move on?

133
00:10:19.107 --> 00:10:26.562
Any stories that they told you or passed down to you that you want to

134
00:10:26.562 --> 00:10:32.527
<c.p>make sure gets on the record for your own grandchildren and children?

135
00:10:32.527 --> 00:10:36.006
<v LEMELLE>You mean from my grandmother and grandfather?

136
00:10:36.006 --> 00:10:37.000
<v CRAMER>Sure.

137
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:45.602
<v LEMELLE>Well, Ira Pots Towner . . . Man, there's so many stories that I could tell you about our grandmother.

138
00:10:45.602 --> 00:10:53.698
And I guess the things that mostly we remember . . . I remember about her more than anything,

139
00:10:53.698 --> 00:10:57.746
was because she was always a homebound person.

140
00:10:57.746 --> 00:11:02.299
You never did see her going out too much.

141
00:11:02.299 --> 00:11:11.407
She did a little . . . When we were small she did a lot of things outside with my grandfather:

142
00:11:11.407 --> 00:11:16.467
building chicken coops and helping him repair the hog pen.

143
00:11:16.467 --> 00:11:23.045
But then in her latter days, grandmother was more of a homebound person.

144
00:11:23.045 --> 00:11:27.599
She stayed more like to herself in her room.

145
00:11:27.599 --> 00:11:32.153
I don't care what time of day you went,

146
00:11:32.153 --> 00:11:41.260
she always had her radio on listening to some type of gospel preacher, someone reading scripture, explaining scripture.

147
00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:52.898
And she had scripture, and if you went to spend the night with her you had to read scripture or listen to scripture.

148
00:11:52.898 --> 00:12:03.524
Sometimes we would, my cousin and I Rogers, we would play like we were asleep so we wouldn't have to read.

149
00:12:03.524 --> 00:12:06.560
That's just the way she was.

150
00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:15.668
And what I remember about her was anytime you went, even when our son was a little boy,

151
00:12:15.668 --> 00:12:26.799
you bring the kids over and she'd always have a little chair for them to sit in and tell you a story.

152
00:12:26.799 --> 00:12:31.859
She always had stories, and most of them were biblical.

153
00:12:31.859 --> 00:12:35.907
She didn’t talk about anything else but God.

154
00:12:35.907 --> 00:12:40.967
That was her . . . God and family, that was her thing.

155
00:12:40.967 --> 00:12:50.075
I think I told someone, at least I told my pastor, we learned from my grandmother and grandfather.

156
00:12:50.075 --> 00:13:01.713
It was a life . . . If we lived according to what she taught us it would be very few errors that we would make.

157
00:13:01.713 --> 00:13:03.231
It would be

158
00:13:03.231 --> 00:13:06.266
<c.p>very few arguments with your spouse.

159
00:13:06.266 --> 00:13:12.338
That's something I never heard my grandmother and grandfather cross-talk each other.

160
00:13:12.338 --> 00:13:17.398
It was . . . If they did, it was behind closed doors.

161
00:13:17.398 --> 00:13:26.000
And they always seemed to be two people that was like two peas in a pod.

162
00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:30.040
It's one thing that always stuck with me.

163
00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:35.594
I told you the story about my grandfather and the trees,

164
00:13:35.594 --> 00:13:48.723
but my grandmother . . . One of the first scriptures I learned was in the book of Proverbs when Solomon . . . The word that Grandmother taught us was that,

165
00:13:48.723 --> 00:13:54.277
“I've never seen the righteous forsaken nor her seed beg bread.”

166
00:13:54.277 --> 00:13:59.832
This was one of her favorite things she would tell us.

167
00:13:59.832 --> 00:14:12.455
And she said, "Long as you trust and believe in God and live right . . . I never seen the righteous forsaken nor the seed beg bread."

168
00:14:12.455 --> 00:14:22.050
We used to always think it as being . . . when you say bread the first thing we thought was eating.

169
00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:27.099
And as we got older she would explain it differently.

170
00:14:27.099 --> 00:14:32.653
That if you are righteous, she said, "Forget about material bread.

171
00:14:32.653 --> 00:14:38.713
What I'm speaking about is the bread of life through Christ Jesus.

172
00:14:38.713 --> 00:14:45.782
If you're righteous, God won't forsake you with the bread of feeding your souls.”

173
00:14:45.782 --> 00:14:57.396
I'm trying to think how many years ago it’s been that the property was owned by Kim and she donated for the church.

174
00:14:57.396 --> 00:15:07.495
This is a real true story, and I can take you there now and show you where the home was.

175
00:15:07.495 --> 00:15:15.069
And it was so . . . I'm going to use the word . . . it was so spiritually divine.

176
00:15:15.069 --> 00:15:26.683
The property was so, to me, was so holy because that's all my grandmother and grandfather ever did was live a holy life.

177
00:15:26.683 --> 00:15:29.713
They preached it; they lived it.

178
00:15:29.713 --> 00:15:31.228
Preached an example.

179
00:15:31.228 --> 00:15:41.327
If my grandmother told you something . . . If the girls . . . If you shouldn't do this, you’d never see her doing it.

180
00:15:41.327 --> 00:15:46.881
Grandfather, if he told you, you’d never seen him doing it.

181
00:15:46.881 --> 00:15:54.960
When we got ready, when my pastor who's still my pastor, Lionel Thierry, started the demolition.

182
00:15:54.960 --> 00:16:00.515
I'm going to call it the demolition of the old home.

183
00:16:00.515 --> 00:16:04.050
And it was to be hauled off.

184
00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:08.594
And he paced and paced and he was praying.

185
00:16:08.594 --> 00:16:13.644
He told me, "Uncle Butch, I prayed and I prayed."

186
00:16:13.644 --> 00:16:14.149
And

187
00:16:14.149 --> 00:16:20.713
<c.p>he said, "For some reason it's hard for me to destroy this home."

188
00:16:20.713 --> 00:16:26.267
He said, "I wouldn't dare destroy it and haul it away."

189
00:16:26.267 --> 00:16:36.871
They had some trucks that was supposed to . . . They were going to load it up and take it, my grandmother's home.

190
00:16:36.871 --> 00:16:40.406
He refused to let them take it.

191
00:16:40.406 --> 00:16:49.495
He dug a hole on the property behind the house, tore it down himself, and buried every piece.

192
00:16:49.495 --> 00:16:56.564
And if we go there now you can see where over time it's sinking.

193
00:16:56.564 --> 00:17:03.129
He put it right behind the church that he was going to build.

194
00:17:03.129 --> 00:17:12.723
And over a period of time . . . I went to look yesterday, I think I went back there, just me.

195
00:17:12.723 --> 00:17:24.842
And I used to tell my pastor, "See my grandmother used to say that this place would always be a place for the bread."

196
00:17:24.842 --> 00:17:30.901
And, oh man, I tell people, "My grandmother . . . she was a woman.

197
00:17:30.901 --> 00:17:32.921
A true, true woman."

198
00:17:32.921 --> 00:17:34.941
You remember Grandmother, baby.

199
00:17:34.941 --> 00:17:41.000
I don't know what else I can say about my grandmother.

200
00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:45.274
<v CRAMER>What's going to happen to that property?

201
00:17:45.274 --> 00:17:51.989
<v LEMELLE>The property belongs to Sasol [South Africa Synthetic Oil Liquid] now.

202
00:17:51.989 --> 00:17:55.042
What they going to do?

203
00:17:55.042 --> 00:17:57.484
I have no idea.

204
00:17:57.484 --> 00:18:08.474
But I do know that there have been quite a few souls saved, baptized in Jesus’ name there.

205
00:18:08.474 --> 00:18:20.074
Whatever Sasol is going to do I feel like God has already done what he said he would do.

206
00:18:20.074 --> 00:18:24.958
The other peculiar . . . because we are peculiar people.

207
00:18:24.958 --> 00:18:48.768
The other thing that stood out to me was once the church was erected and was ready for fellowship . . . If you would have gone there I could show you that when you walk into the entrance of the building,

208
00:18:48.768 --> 00:19:04.032
which we call a lobby or foyer, when you walk into it, it was almost like walking into my grandmother's front door of her house.

209
00:19:04.032 --> 00:19:07.084
When you would . . . Oh geez.

210
00:19:07.084 --> 00:19:07.284
I can't do it.

211
00:19:07.084 --> 00:19:09.526
<v LEONA LEMELLE>[. . . ?]

212
00:19:09.526 --> 00:19:15.632
<v LEMELLE>Baby . . . I guess what's so unique when you came in,

213
00:19:15.632 --> 00:19:25.400
when you would take a right turn after coming into the church foyer you come in,

214
00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:38.832
and when you would turn right that was about where my pastor's office was that he bring in for counseling or teaching,

215
00:19:38.832 --> 00:19:46.158
was within two or three foot from where my grandmother's bedroom was.

216
00:19:46.158 --> 00:20:11.189
She had us . . . It was all just so unique that particular spot and was where she had us constantly . . . Because that was the only part of the house that . . . unless she would go in the kitchen to fix toast or something.

217
00:20:11.189 --> 00:20:25.232
It was so unique that this particular spot would be the place where he chose to be, like one-on-one teacher or one-on-one conversation.

218
00:20:25.232 --> 00:20:35.000
That's the way my grandmother had one-on-one with us or conversation with several of us.

219
00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:40.960
And the other peculiar, not peculiar, the other . . . I call it spiritual.

220
00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:51.390
God just . . . I think God put everything in place when . . . I told you the story about the trees and the burrs.

221
00:20:51.390 --> 00:21:00.827
When you would go into the sanctuary and where my grandfather used to sit and we had the trees,

222
00:21:00.827 --> 00:21:18.707
where my grandfather used to sit and teach us and talk is exactly where my pastor ended up putting the pulpit that he stands at the pulpit and teach and we down below looking at him.

223
00:21:18.707 --> 00:21:29.137
And I just always thought that was amazing the way it ended up, that my grandmother's words yet still goes on.

224
00:21:29.137 --> 00:21:32.614
The church itself was named Mossville Truth.

225
00:21:32.614 --> 00:21:39.071
Even though we moved to Lake Charles, guess what the church’s name is?

226
00:21:39.071 --> 00:21:40.064
Mossville Truth.

227
00:21:40.064 --> 00:21:43.044
That's why I'm a truth seeker.

228
00:21:43.044 --> 00:21:46.024
So I got so many stories.

229
00:21:46.024 --> 00:21:52.481
But that's one of the main things I can remember about my grandmother.

230
00:21:52.481 --> 00:21:54.468
She was so sweet.

231
00:21:54.468 --> 00:22:01.421
And I'm a . . . I want to say she loved all of us very dearly.

232
00:22:01.421 --> 00:22:08.375
But I still believe I had a special, special place in my grandmother's heart.

233
00:22:08.375 --> 00:22:09.865
I was working.

234
00:22:09.865 --> 00:22:17.315
At that time, I was working at Conoco and my grandmother . . . My wife called me.

235
00:22:17.315 --> 00:22:25.262
I think you the one that called me when my grandmother . . . My wife’s memory is not

236
00:22:25.262 --> 00:22:27.745
<c.p>like it used to be.

237
00:22:27.745 --> 00:22:35.692
But called and told me that my grandmother was at home and she was real sick.

238
00:22:35.692 --> 00:22:38.672
She was ill for some reason.

239
00:22:38.672 --> 00:22:49.102
And my supervisor came and got me, and I came home and went straight to her house to see about her.

240
00:22:49.102 --> 00:22:57.545
And my aunty and I, we loaded up in my car and brought her to the hospital.

241
00:22:57.545 --> 00:23:05.492
When we brought her in, they rushed and rushed and they had her in the bed.

242
00:23:05.492 --> 00:23:15.426
It was me and my [?] niece and she told the nurse she wanted to see me if I was there.

243
00:23:15.426 --> 00:23:18.902
And so I went in her room.

244
00:23:18.902 --> 00:23:27.346
I'm the last person that kissed her and the last person she hugged, me and my aunty.

245
00:23:27.346 --> 00:23:32.313
I've never seen death come so easy, and so relaxed.

246
00:23:32.313 --> 00:23:34.796
I never heard her moan.

247
00:23:34.796 --> 00:23:37.279
I never heard her groan.

248
00:23:37.279 --> 00:23:44.729
All she did was when I hugged her and told her, "Grandmother, I love you."

249
00:23:44.729 --> 00:23:47.213
She said, "I love you."

250
00:23:47.213 --> 00:23:51.683
And she did like that, closed her little eyes.

251
00:23:51.683 --> 00:23:55.656
Just as peaceful as we're sitting in here.

252
00:23:55.656 --> 00:23:57.146
Quiet and peaceful.

253
00:23:57.146 --> 00:24:05.093
And that was the first time they ever actually seen . . . that I actually ever seen death,

254
00:24:05.093 --> 00:24:10.557
and it wasn't no something to be afraid of or hollered.

255
00:24:10.557 --> 00:24:12.543
It was just peaceful.

256
00:24:12.543 --> 00:24:16.517
She closed her eyes, and that was it.

257
00:24:16.517 --> 00:24:19.000
That was my grandmother.

258
00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:23.934
And the same thing, I guess, happened with my mother when she passed.

259
00:24:23.934 --> 00:24:31.904
I was at the hospital and she was worrying and worrying and when we . . . my sister Della and I was there.

260
00:24:31.904 --> 00:24:38.355
And when I got to her bed and I said, reminded me a lot of her mother.

261
00:24:38.355 --> 00:24:46.325
And I told her, I said, "Mama, if you trying to hang on here for us, we going to be fine.

262
00:24:46.325 --> 00:24:49.361
You just go on home and meet Jesus."

263
00:24:49.361 --> 00:24:52.398
And my little mother did the same thing.

264
00:24:52.398 --> 00:24:57.331
She took a deep breath and closed her, her eyes was already closed.

265
00:24:57.331 --> 00:25:02.265
So I just think it's all because of the life that they lived.

266
00:25:02.265 --> 00:25:06.819
That's why I said, if we . . . if I, one of her grandsons,

267
00:25:06.819 --> 00:25:12.892
my mother's only son, if I would have lived my life the way they taught us . . .

268
00:25:12.892 --> 00:25:14.030
<v LEONA LEMELLE>You'd be alright.

269
00:25:14.030 --> 00:25:15.928
<v LEMELLE>. . . I'd be skipping to scooby-dooby-doo.

270
00:25:15.928 --> 00:25:17.446
I'd be all right.

271
00:25:17.446 --> 00:25:20.861
Would have been all right a long time ago.

272
00:25:20.861 --> 00:25:22.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yes, Lord.

273
00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:23.116
<v LEMELLE>We've made errors.

274
00:25:23.116 --> 00:25:30.556
I'll never say I never made an error or did things wrong, but I tried so hard to straighten everything.

275
00:25:30.556 --> 00:25:34.648
Some things you do, like you can't ever pay it back.

276
00:25:34.648 --> 00:25:40.972
If I lived every day of my life now, I could never live enough to pay back.

277
00:25:40.972 --> 00:25:48.040
But my pastor tell me all the time he say, "Uncle Butch"– everybody calls me Uncle Butch – "Uncle Butch,

278
00:25:48.040 --> 00:25:53.248
you have to just let all that go and live for your life now."

279
00:25:53.248 --> 00:26:02.548
<v CRAMER>Well I bet there are people in your life who probably feel the same way about you that you do with your mom and your . . .

280
00:26:02.548 --> 00:26:03.664
<v LEMELLE>I hope so.

281
00:26:03.664 --> 00:26:06.268
Least some of them say they do.

282
00:26:06.268 --> 00:26:08.872
I don't know if they mean it.

283
00:26:08.872 --> 00:26:11.104
Some of them says they do.

284
00:26:11.104 --> 00:26:12.964
<v CRAMER>I bet they mean it.

285
00:26:12.964 --> 00:26:16.684
So where exactly was the house where you grew up?

286
00:26:16.684 --> 00:26:18.916
What was the address of it?

287
00:26:18.916 --> 00:26:23.008
Where is it in relation to where we are right now?

288
00:26:23.008 --> 00:26:25.612
<v LEMELLE>In relationship to where we are now,

289
00:26:25.612 --> 00:26:34.540
if you were in the city it's about two and a half blocks away to the west of here on the Old Spanish Trail.

290
00:26:34.540 --> 00:26:36.028
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Right down the street.

291
00:26:36.028 --> 00:26:39.748
<v LEMELLE>It's just the first house when you pass Prater Road.

292
00:26:39.748 --> 00:26:41.236
It still yet stands.

293
00:26:41.236 --> 00:26:43.096
You've passed it several times.

294
00:26:43.096 --> 00:26:45.328
I think you went to it.

295
00:26:45.328 --> 00:26:46.444
You went there.

296
00:26:46.444 --> 00:26:52.024
<v CRAMER>I went to your mom's house . . . where you grew up at in your mom's house.

297
00:26:52.024 --> 00:26:53.884
<v LEMELLE>Where I grew up at.

298
00:26:53.884 --> 00:26:55.000
<v CRAMER>That's right.

299
00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:00.175
<v LEMELLE>And across the street was where my grandmother's house was.

300
00:27:00.175 --> 00:27:02.246
It's no longer there.

301
00:27:02.246 --> 00:27:05.868
<v CRAMER>It's exactly . . . it’s on Old Spanish Trail.

302
00:27:05.868 --> 00:27:06.386
<v LEMELLE>Right.

303
00:27:06.386 --> 00:27:06.904
<v CRAMER>Right.

304
00:27:06.904 --> 00:27:13.114
So can you tell us a little bit about Old Spanish Trail?

305
00:27:13.114 --> 00:27:16.737
I mean, it's got such a history.

306
00:27:16.737 --> 00:27:20.360
It goes all over the United States.

307
00:27:20.360 --> 00:27:27.088
What about . . . Because when you were . . . It's probably changed a lot since you . . .

308
00:27:27.088 --> 00:27:29.158
<v LEMELLE>Oh my god, yes.

309
00:27:29.158 --> 00:27:29.675
<v CRAMER>Right?

310
00:27:29.675 --> 00:27:30.193
Yeah.

311
00:27:30.193 --> 00:27:33.298
<v LEMELLE>Let's say the Old Spanish Trail.

312
00:27:33.298 --> 00:27:45.202
Of course, when I got big enough to be turned loose down there, the Old Spanish Trail, it was a small gravel road.

313
00:27:45.202 --> 00:27:51.412
Later on . . . Because they say it used to be a brick road.

314
00:27:51.412 --> 00:27:54.518
I can't remember that being brick.

315
00:27:54.518 --> 00:27:58.658
I remember like a rock and gravel road.

316
00:27:58.658 --> 00:28:09.009
And I guess somewhere in the early '50s is when they kind of start redoing it, making a little asphalt.

317
00:28:09.009 --> 00:28:14.184
And as time passed the roads got wider and wider.

318
00:28:14.184 --> 00:28:21.947
But the Old Spanish Trail was probably one of the main travel roads through here.

319
00:28:21.947 --> 00:28:26.605
Before Highway 90 it was the Old Spanish Trail.

320
00:28:26.605 --> 00:28:32.298
<v LEONA LEMELLE>It would go all the way up into Houston [Texas], California.

321
00:28:32.298 --> 00:28:37.474
I think it go all the way up into California.

322
00:28:37.474 --> 00:28:37.991
<v LEMELLE>OST.

323
00:28:37.991 --> 00:28:43.684
Because some places you'll see . . . Even in Houston, you'll see OST.

324
00:28:43.684 --> 00:28:44.202
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah.

325
00:28:44.202 --> 00:28:48.860
<v LEMELLE>And that was part of the Old Spanish Trail.

326
00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:53.000
<v CRAMER>It goes all the way to Florida.

327
00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:56.308
<v LEMELLE>It goes all the way to Florida.

328
00:28:56.308 --> 00:29:06.706
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I think about the wagon train came through here, the Indians. [Talk about?] dead bones all up under there in this place.

329
00:29:06.706 --> 00:29:10.488
<v LEMELLE>You're not going to get to the gypsies.

330
00:29:10.488 --> 00:29:13.323
But it was a main travel.

331
00:29:13.323 --> 00:29:23.249
And I toot the horn for my parents and grandparents because this . . . Well the community itself, that's the way they were.

332
00:29:23.249 --> 00:29:27.975
This was the main place, the main place to pass.

333
00:29:27.975 --> 00:29:29.393
Strangers passed through.

334
00:29:29.393 --> 00:29:33.174
If they needed help, they would help them.

335
00:29:33.174 --> 00:29:36.483
Give them a place, something to eat.

336
00:29:36.483 --> 00:29:38.373
Campout in their yard.

337
00:29:38.373 --> 00:29:40.264
Things of that nature.

338
00:29:40.264 --> 00:29:47.353
I can actually remember, probably when I was about nine, eight or nine years old,

339
00:29:47.353 --> 00:29:53.498
they used to have the wagons pass through, and we were all frightened.

340
00:29:53.498 --> 00:30:01.060
There was two things that we were afraid of: what they call a hobo and gypsies.

341
00:30:01.060 --> 00:30:07.204
You could hear the wagon coming and it would be some little bells.

342
00:30:07.204 --> 00:30:12.876
And that was time for us to scatter because we didn't know.

343
00:30:12.876 --> 00:30:16.657
And this was gypsies passing through . . . actually gypsies.

344
00:30:16.657 --> 00:30:28.000
They'd have their wagon, and they'd have some kind of bells hanging on the wagon that would just be [makes bell noise] ding-a-ling-a-ling.

345
00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:37.650
And my grandmother and them used to say that was to let you know that they wasn't trying to slip up on you,

346
00:30:37.650 --> 00:30:39.328
that they were harmless.

347
00:30:39.328 --> 00:30:45.621
They were just people travelling from the east because everyone one was saying, "Go west.

348
00:30:45.621 --> 00:30:46.461
Go west."

349
00:30:46.461 --> 00:30:46.880
Gold.

350
00:30:46.880 --> 00:30:50.656
People left going looking for gold going out west.

351
00:30:50.656 --> 00:30:57.789
I can't tell you how many wagons passed through here that probably ended up out toward California.

352
00:30:57.789 --> 00:31:01.565
But the gypsies’ wagons would be like loaded, man.

353
00:31:01.565 --> 00:31:04.502
And they'd be making a little ding-a-ling-a-ling.

354
00:31:04.502 --> 00:31:15.410
And they'd go so far some of the people would tell them, "Okay, you can camp out here for the night,” whatever, and give them food.

355
00:31:15.410 --> 00:31:22.543
We have a joke about my sister Julia. [Laughs] I'll let her tell you about that one.

356
00:31:22.543 --> 00:31:22.962
Julia

357
00:31:22.962 --> 00:31:31.773
<c.p>say they have some gypsies came . . . They used to say that the gypsies or someone had treasures buried in certain places,

358
00:31:31.773 --> 00:31:39.744
and right next door . . . right out front by my mother's house they claimed that there was some treasures buried.

359
00:31:39.744 --> 00:31:45.199
And someone was trying to dig up the treasures and they heard [black?].

360
00:31:45.199 --> 00:31:49.394
These people will tell you all kinds of old stories,

361
00:31:49.394 --> 00:32:00.303
but Julia say they had a gypsy one time came and she saw the gypsy go up in the tree and it never did come out.

362
00:32:00.303 --> 00:32:01.562
I said, "Wow."

363
00:32:01.562 --> 00:32:04.918
The gypsy . . . [Laughs] Take this off the record.

364
00:32:04.918 --> 00:32:10.372
I told them, "Julia was such a terrible girl and she's totally . . ." Now,

365
00:32:10.372 --> 00:32:15.826
I’m not going to say that about my sister because I don't know.

366
00:32:15.826 --> 00:32:17.924
I told my sister Della.

367
00:32:17.924 --> 00:32:24.218
I say, "Julia saw a gypsy go up in the tree and didn’t come back.”

368
00:32:24.218 --> 00:32:27.155
I said, “I know what happened now."

369
00:32:27.155 --> 00:32:28.413
Della say, "What?"

370
00:32:28.413 --> 00:32:41.000
I said, "I really believe that gypsy was her mama and throwed her little bad butt away." [Laughs] And my mother just felt sorry for her and raised her.

371
00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:47.125
But the Old Spanish Trail seriously was . . . well from history and a little part I remember,

372
00:32:47.125 --> 00:32:51.719
this was the main drag because people, business people, lumber, different thing.

373
00:32:51.719 --> 00:32:57.844
You would see them come through with their wagons headed towards Sulphur [Louisiana] to sulfur mines.

374
00:32:57.844 --> 00:33:03.969
A lot of construction went through here and they built it up and called it Brimstone,

375
00:33:03.969 --> 00:33:08.562
which was another pathway to kind of ease the traffic, I guess.

376
00:33:08.562 --> 00:33:12.391
And the wagons stopped traveling here and more automobiles started.

377
00:33:12.391 --> 00:33:21.961
They used to travel back in the back . . . just a little piece back here about a hundred yards in an old place called the Brimstone,

378
00:33:21.961 --> 00:33:25.789
and it ran from here all the way to Sulphur.

379
00:33:25.789 --> 00:33:26.555
The Brimstone.

380
00:33:26.555 --> 00:33:30.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>That go all the way to Texas, huh?

381
00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:39.456
<v LEMELLE>And it . . . I'm not sure how far it goes, but it goes up to the old part of

382
00:33:39.456 --> 00:33:42.082
<c.p>Sulphur to the sulfur mines.

383
00:33:42.082 --> 00:33:45.759
The Brimstone runs to the sulfur mines.

384
00:33:45.759 --> 00:33:58.892
There was a lot of construction for sulfur mines, so to eliminate OST they built the right of way and they call it the Brimstone.

385
00:33:58.892 --> 00:34:00.994
It goes . . . oh, Lord.

386
00:34:00.994 --> 00:34:05.722
I know it's about a hundred yards behind here.

387
00:34:05.722 --> 00:34:08.873
But nice, all pipelined and everything.

388
00:34:08.873 --> 00:34:12.551
And business in Westlake [Louisiana] used this,

389
00:34:12.551 --> 00:34:26.734
and also families here used the OST to go back and forth to Westlake Grocery because Prater Road was named from my mother's sister's husband, [Earthy?] Prater.

390
00:34:26.734 --> 00:34:33.563
Mr. [Amara?] Prater's father, he started building a road, a right of way,

391
00:34:33.563 --> 00:34:48.797
and I guess with the work that he did putting down and making a right of way from Old Spanish Trail up to Highway 90 when they started it,

392
00:34:48.797 --> 00:34:50.373
they changed that . . .

393
00:34:50.373 --> 00:34:53.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I didn’t know that.

394
00:34:53.000 --> 00:35:05.188
<v LEMELLE>That's how Prater Road got its name because of Earthy Prater was the man that started right of way for access to go to . . . Because once they built Highway 90,

395
00:35:05.188 --> 00:35:13.719
the Old Spanish Trail wasn't as busy, but you still needed a way to get to Maplewood where their jobs were.

396
00:35:13.719 --> 00:35:19.812
So he started building a right of way, again I say, Earthy Prater, our uncle.

397
00:35:19.812 --> 00:35:24.281
And then people in the community fell in and helped him.

398
00:35:24.281 --> 00:35:24.688
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Okay.

399
00:35:24.688 --> 00:35:27.125
<v LEMELLE>And eventually what happened, I guess,

400
00:35:27.125 --> 00:35:39.719
eventually the parish or whomever got involved in it and actually made a . . . Because they saw then that it was a big help to get back and forth to Cities Service,

401
00:35:39.719 --> 00:35:40.938
to the refineries.

402
00:35:40.938 --> 00:35:42.156
All that area.

403
00:35:42.156 --> 00:35:46.219
<v CRAMER>So he probably had to cut down the trees and . . .

404
00:35:46.219 --> 00:35:47.031
<v LEMELLE>Oh, yes.

405
00:35:47.031 --> 00:35:50.281
He had to do a lot of work.

406
00:35:50.281 --> 00:35:55.562
<v CRAMER>How do you . . . What kind of equipment did he use to do that?

407
00:35:55.562 --> 00:35:55.969
Saw?

408
00:35:55.969 --> 00:35:58.000
What about mules or . . .?

409
00:35:58.000 --> 00:36:00.210
<v LEMELLE>He had . . . I never forget.

410
00:36:00.210 --> 00:36:03.305
My uncle had a horse named Silver.

411
00:36:03.305 --> 00:36:03.747
Wagon.

412
00:36:03.747 --> 00:36:04.631
Old Silver.

413
00:36:04.631 --> 00:36:08.609
But this was before my time, about the road.

414
00:36:08.609 --> 00:36:15.682
But I know he had . . . What’s that old man's name . . . Mr. Ben Hartman was a neighbor.

415
00:36:15.682 --> 00:36:21.871
And those old men, they would get out work and they had the horses.

416
00:36:21.871 --> 00:36:31.597
I can't remember a mule, but they had horses and they had wagons and they had another thing they called a sled,

417
00:36:31.597 --> 00:36:33.365
I think . . . a sled.

418
00:36:33.365 --> 00:36:38.227
They put a lot of things on there and the horse.

419
00:36:38.227 --> 00:36:46.627
And that's how the hauled a lot of . . . And then a lot of the timber went to my grandfather.

420
00:36:46.627 --> 00:36:48.395
He cut it up.

421
00:36:48.395 --> 00:36:49.721
He’d burn it.

422
00:36:49.721 --> 00:36:51.047
He had coal.

423
00:36:51.047 --> 00:36:56.794
So they was all in cahoots to how to . . . I call it survival.

424
00:36:56.794 --> 00:37:08.288
A lot of the timber that Earthy and them cut, they loaded it up on the wagon or the sled and brought it to my grandfather.

425
00:37:08.288 --> 00:37:18.897
At that time my grandfather was living up on Evergreen [Road], and that's where he did a lot of his work, up on Evergreen.

426
00:37:18.897 --> 00:37:28.180
And then when his home burnt down up on Evergreen, this is when he moved up on the Old Spanish Trail.

427
00:37:28.180 --> 00:37:37.906
But a lot of his work . . . Can you imagine hauling timber from Old Prater Road all the way up there by horse?

428
00:37:37.906 --> 00:37:41.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>And didn't they come from Sugartown?

429
00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:41.371
<v LEMELLE>Yeah.

430
00:37:41.371 --> 00:37:45.086
<v LEONA LEMELLE>And it would take so many days to get here.

431
00:37:45.086 --> 00:37:46.943
<v LEMELLE>In Sugartown . . . Sugartown is just . . .

432
00:37:46.943 --> 00:37:48.057
<v LEONA LEMELLE>For a horse . . .

433
00:37:48.057 --> 00:37:50.286
<v LEMELLE>Just before you get to Deridder.

434
00:37:50.286 --> 00:37:54.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>They had a horse and a wagon, I guess.

435
00:37:54.000 --> 00:37:57.000
<v LEMELLE>That's how my mother came here with her father.

436
00:37:57.000 --> 00:38:00.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>It would take a long time to get here.

437
00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:02.333
<v LEMELLE>She remembers camping out under the stars.

438
00:38:02.333 --> 00:38:04.667
Traveling what takes me thirty, forty-five minutes.

439
00:38:04.667 --> 00:38:06.667
<v LEONA LEMELLE>[Want me to talk about Paw-Paw?]

440
00:38:06.667 --> 00:38:07.333
<v LEMELLE>Forty-five minutes.

441
00:38:07.333 --> 00:38:09.333
When she ask for something, baby.

442
00:38:09.333 --> 00:38:10.667
<v CRAMER>Okay, talk about Paw-Paw.

443
00:38:10.667 --> 00:38:11.333
<v LEMELLE>It's fine.

444
00:38:11.333 --> 00:38:12.333
<v CRAMER>What about Paw-Paw?

445
00:38:12.333 --> 00:38:13.000
<v LEMELLE>It's fine.

446
00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:14.000
Who was Paw-Paw?

447
00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:15.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>His daddy.

448
00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:17.794
<v LEMELLE>We on my grandparents now.

449
00:38:17.794 --> 00:38:18.353
<v CRAMER>Well . . .

450
00:38:18.353 --> 00:38:25.059
<v LEMELLE>All I can remember that . . . Again, now my grandmother, Ira Pots Thomas,

451
00:38:25.059 --> 00:38:32.882
she had one brother that I knew of and his name was Elic Pots.

452
00:38:32.882 --> 00:38:38.471
And he treated us all like . . . What's better than gold?

453
00:38:38.471 --> 00:38:40.147
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Where he live?

454
00:38:40.147 --> 00:38:41.265
<v LEMELLE>Uncle Pots.

455
00:38:41.265 --> 00:38:42.941
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Oh, in Texas.

456
00:38:42.941 --> 00:38:49.647
<v LEMELLE>No, no. He lived down the road where Margie's place was at.

457
00:38:49.647 --> 00:38:53.000
Uncle Pots and Aunt Lily.

458
00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:55.755
<v CRAMER>How did you spell his name?

459
00:38:55.755 --> 00:38:56.673
E-L-I-C-H or . . .?

460
00:38:56.673 --> 00:38:57.133
<v LEMELLE>Who?

461
00:38:57.133 --> 00:38:58.969
<v CRAMER>You said Uncle Elic.

462
00:38:58.969 --> 00:38:59.429
<v LEMELLE>Elic.

463
00:38:59.429 --> 00:39:02.184
E-F- . . . Now wait a minute now.

464
00:39:02.184 --> 00:39:02.643
Elic.

465
00:39:02.643 --> 00:39:04.020
E-L-I-C or E-L-I-K.

466
00:39:04.020 --> 00:39:04.480
Elic.

467
00:39:04.480 --> 00:39:04.939
<v CRAMER>Okay.

468
00:39:04.939 --> 00:39:11.367
<v LEMELLE>Elic Pots was my grandmother's brother, and he was one that loved his nieces.

469
00:39:11.367 --> 00:39:23.306
He didn't care for any kids, but every niece and nephew he treat us like we were gold because he loved all of his sister's grandkids.

470
00:39:23.306 --> 00:39:31.571
And my wife remembered the other brother and sisters that my grandfather . . . They lived up in Houston [Texas].

471
00:39:31.571 --> 00:39:33.867
One was named Gibson Towner.

472
00:39:33.867 --> 00:39:38.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>That's your daddy people or your mama people?

473
00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:39.682
<v LEMELLE>We on my grandfather.

474
00:39:39.682 --> 00:39:40.102
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Grandpa.

475
00:39:40.102 --> 00:39:41.364
I mean, grandma.

476
00:39:41.364 --> 00:39:44.307
That's what I was trying to say.

477
00:39:44.307 --> 00:39:46.830
<v LEMELLE>No. We’re on Grandpa Duff, baby.

478
00:39:46.830 --> 00:39:47.250
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Okay.

479
00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:47.670
<v LEMELLE>Towner.

480
00:39:47.670 --> 00:39:49.773
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I didn't know him, sorry.

481
00:39:49.773 --> 00:39:50.193
<v LEMELLE>Yeah.

482
00:39:50.193 --> 00:39:53.136
<v CRAMER>Now how do you spell Earthy Prater?

483
00:39:53.136 --> 00:39:55.239
How do you spell that?

484
00:39:55.239 --> 00:39:56.500
Do you know?

485
00:39:56.500 --> 00:39:59.443
<v LEMELLE>E . . . Erthy[?] . . . E-R-T-H-Y, I guess [spelled Earthy].

486
00:39:59.443 --> 00:39:59.864
<v CRAMER>Okay.

487
00:39:59.864 --> 00:40:04.489
<v LEMELLE>I could get my phone and find out, but Earthy Prater.

488
00:40:04.489 --> 00:40:12.477
And you going to get that . . . You’ll probably talk to . . . Who from that family we was trying to get?

489
00:40:12.477 --> 00:40:13.318
Tommy Prater?

490
00:40:13.318 --> 00:40:15.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>That other one.

491
00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:15.349
<v CRAMER>Morris?

492
00:40:15.349 --> 00:40:20.590
<v LEMELLE>We was trying to get Morris and Tommy, but neither one of them won’t come.

493
00:40:20.590 --> 00:40:25.133
Now Vera . . . But I probably know as much about them as Vera would.

494
00:40:25.133 --> 00:40:27.229
<v CRAMER>So you mentioned the sulfur mines.

495
00:40:27.229 --> 00:40:32.120
Do you have memories of the sulfur mines, or was that before your time?

496
00:40:32.120 --> 00:40:34.566
<v LEMELLE>It was more like before my time.

497
00:40:34.566 --> 00:40:37.012
<v CRAMER>But you heard about them I’d guess.

498
00:40:37.012 --> 00:40:37.711
<v LEMELLE>Oh yes.

499
00:40:37.711 --> 00:40:40.157
<v CRAMER>Tell us about how close those were.

500
00:40:40.157 --> 00:40:44.000
And were people in Mossville working at the sulfur mines?

501
00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:47.483
<v LEMELLE>Now that part I'm not really sure.

502
00:40:47.483 --> 00:40:50.966
Most likely some of the people did.

503
00:40:50.966 --> 00:40:58.429
But back to Earthy Prater, he was commended greatly for that work that he did.

504
00:40:58.429 --> 00:41:07.883
And this gave them access to getting across and getting out to where the refineries, such as Cities Service,

505
00:41:07.883 --> 00:41:17.337
Firestone started construction of those back in the '30s, which my father, different ones like Uncle Earthy, they worked.

506
00:41:17.337 --> 00:41:26.790
Uncle Earthy ended up with a good job back in them days because he went to work first construction.

507
00:41:26.790 --> 00:41:36.741
Then Uncle Earthy worked at Firestone, which was . . . back then that was a great job for a person, you know.

508
00:41:36.741 --> 00:41:49.180
But the sulfur mines, I don't know of anyone personally that I could really do to say and be honest that I knew worked there.

509
00:41:49.180 --> 00:41:58.137
<v CRAMER>Because I think that was, if my memory serves me correctly, around the turn of the 20th century.

510
00:41:58.137 --> 00:42:00.127
<v LEMELLE>It was back then.

511
00:42:00.127 --> 00:42:02.117
<v CRAMER>And didn't last long.

512
00:42:02.117 --> 00:42:03.610
<v LEMELLE>Didn't last long.

513
00:42:03.610 --> 00:42:06.098
<v CRAMER>I guess lumber had already . . .

514
00:42:06.098 --> 00:42:06.595
<v LEMELLE>Right.

515
00:42:06.595 --> 00:42:07.093
<v CRAMER>. . . happened.

516
00:42:07.093 --> 00:42:12.566
But there was a sawmill for a little while though, right?

517
00:42:12.566 --> 00:42:15.054
<v LEMELLE>Yeah, they had a sawmill.

518
00:42:15.054 --> 00:42:19.532
Now they had several people here that had sawmills.

519
00:42:19.532 --> 00:42:26.000
But that's a little . . . Some of that's a little before my time.

520
00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:30.524
<v CRAMER>Because it was mostly . . . I mean, after World War II we had lots of industries come up in Mossville.

521
00:42:30.524 --> 00:42:31.000
<v LEMELLE>Right, right.

522
00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:32.667
<v CRAMER>So what were some of those industries?

523
00:42:32.667 --> 00:42:34.571
I mean, I know you were a kid.

524
00:42:34.571 --> 00:42:36.000
You were just one, but . . .

525
00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:45.396
<v LEMELLE>Okay, I have papers where my father worked for . . . I'm trying to think of the name of these companies.

526
00:42:45.396 --> 00:42:46.879
Like, construction . . . Kellogg.

527
00:42:46.879 --> 00:42:58.253
I remember my dad talking about Kellogg Construction Company that was when they started to build Cities Service and Conoco in the thirties.

528
00:42:58.253 --> 00:43:03.198
And that's when Earthy Prater built . . . I can know this,

529
00:43:03.198 --> 00:43:11.110
that when he came from the stories they told us that Uncle Earthy started that road,

530
00:43:11.110 --> 00:43:18.527
and that gave them access to get easier down to Bayou D'Inde, all that area.

531
00:43:18.527 --> 00:43:19.022
So . . .

532
00:43:19.022 --> 00:43:27.429
<v CRAMER>Now that road, is it the same exact road today, or has it been shortened or changed?

533
00:43:27.429 --> 00:43:36.330
<v LEMELLE>It has been . . . Prater Road, I would say, is extended because it used to go to Highway 90.

534
00:43:36.330 --> 00:43:45.231
Then they made it access all the way over to Bayou D'Inde, and you could take Prater Road.

535
00:43:45.231 --> 00:43:55.615
They named the south side of Prater Road . . . of Highway 90, they named it Prater Road and there are subdivision homes.

536
00:43:55.615 --> 00:44:02.044
And you continue, you can take Prater Road down and hit [Apisay?] . . not [?].

537
00:44:02.044 --> 00:44:06.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>You just said it not long ago.

538
00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:09.094
<v LEMELLE>You can get to [Interstate] 210 from Prater Road.

539
00:44:09.094 --> 00:44:13.562
But all that Prater Road on this side was started by Earthy Prater.

540
00:44:13.562 --> 00:44:15.281
<v CRAMER>It starts right here, right?

541
00:44:15.281 --> 00:44:17.000
At the intersection of . . .

542
00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:19.339
<v LEMELLE>At the intersection of OST.

543
00:44:19.339 --> 00:44:23.082
<v CRAMER>So what are the geographic boundaries of Mossville?

544
00:44:23.082 --> 00:44:27.292
Or what were they when you were growing up?

545
00:44:27.292 --> 00:44:31.503
<v LEMELLE>When I was growing up the boundaries of Mossville,

546
00:44:31.503 --> 00:44:38.520
even though they had . . . In other words, some people say, "Well I'm not from Mossville.

547
00:44:38.520 --> 00:44:40.392
I'm from Bel Air."

548
00:44:40.392 --> 00:44:42.731
But that's part of Mossville.

549
00:44:42.731 --> 00:44:44.602
It's the same thing.

550
00:44:44.602 --> 00:44:53.959
If you were living in Lake Charles and say, "Well I live in Gasport,” you're still part of Lake Charles.

551
00:44:53.959 --> 00:45:05.187
So the boundaries when I was coming up for Mossville was the first . . . the last family to the west of us was the LaTours,

552
00:45:05.187 --> 00:45:11.269
and next door to the LaTours was my mother's oldest brother, Arthur Towner.

553
00:45:11.269 --> 00:45:18.287
That was our boundaries in this time with the Mosses and all of those people.

554
00:45:18.287 --> 00:45:20.158
They built from there.

555
00:45:20.158 --> 00:45:27.643
But our boundaries, believe it or not, used to go all the way up to Conoco.

556
00:45:27.643 --> 00:45:31.854
Part of Conoco’s tank farm used to be Mossville.

557
00:45:31.854 --> 00:45:37.000
A lot of their construction sites used to be Mossville.

558
00:45:37.000 --> 00:45:41.223
<v CRAMER>What do you mean by "used to be Mossville"?

559
00:45:41.223 --> 00:45:44.508
Like there used to be houses there?

560
00:45:44.508 --> 00:45:44.977
<v LEMELLE>Yes.

561
00:45:44.977 --> 00:45:46.385
There were homes.

562
00:45:46.385 --> 00:46:00.462
I can name some of the homes that was . . . If you leave here and go to Conoco Philips or Sasol . . . the tank farm where they keep all the crude oil,

563
00:46:00.462 --> 00:46:10.785
which would be the tanks that you see that's silver, that's to keep the heat, all of that used to be homes.

564
00:46:10.785 --> 00:46:17.823
<c.p>It was the Scotts, the Sullivans, and on down the old Trousdale before they closed,

565
00:46:17.823 --> 00:46:28.146
before companies like Conoco and Sasol purchased it, there was another Trousdale that had, Lord, I don't know, it had families there.

566
00:46:28.146 --> 00:46:35.654
When I was coming up there was a church, Philadelphia Church, which is now back here.

567
00:46:35.654 --> 00:46:38.000
They moved the church.

568
00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:39.448
We had clubs.

569
00:46:39.448 --> 00:46:47.170
We had ball parks that we would go down and watch ball games on Sunday evening.

570
00:46:47.170 --> 00:46:49.100
You met Kenny Lee?

571
00:46:49.100 --> 00:46:54.409
His father played on the team and was a ball player.

572
00:46:54.409 --> 00:46:58.752
So all that used to be part of Mossville.

573
00:46:58.752 --> 00:47:05.509
And across the road from Conoco, where it's now Sasol, was part of Mossville.

574
00:47:05.509 --> 00:47:07.439
They had homes there.

575
00:47:07.439 --> 00:47:14.196
When I was a kid, being a segregated place, they had a beautiful park . . .

576
00:47:14.196 --> 00:47:15.643
<v LEONA LEMELLE>[Mossville Conoco Park?].

577
00:47:15.643 --> 00:47:18.057
<v LEMELLE>They had a beautiful park.

578
00:47:18.057 --> 00:47:24.330
And we had a little bitty park across from . . . I'm just being honest.

579
00:47:24.330 --> 00:47:26.743
That's the way segregation was.

580
00:47:26.743 --> 00:47:37.843
But due to the fact that they had people here worked for Conoco, well Conoco said, "We going to give y'all a park."

581
00:47:37.843 --> 00:47:42.670
So they built a little small park for the blacks.

582
00:47:42.670 --> 00:47:49.426
And all that now is closed, and it's all tank farm or whatever area.

583
00:47:49.426 --> 00:47:55.700
But we had the little park we could go to and have fun.

584
00:47:55.700 --> 00:48:04.387
Mr. Prater, he would take us down there and we'd cook and barbeque at the park, Conoco Park.

585
00:48:04.387 --> 00:48:09.696
Which we never did perceive it being nothing so drastically wrong.

586
00:48:09.696 --> 00:48:17.900
We had ours, and I think somewhere like in 1960 . . . it was before anybody else was integrated,

587
00:48:17.900 --> 00:48:22.726
Conoco integrated the parks, and we were allowed to go.

588
00:48:22.726 --> 00:48:26.587
But all that at one time was homes.

589
00:48:26.587 --> 00:48:29.000
They had families there.

590
00:48:29.000 --> 00:48:34.673
<v CRAMER>So how did they go from being homes to being owned by the

591
00:48:34.673 --> 00:48:35.109
<c.p>companies?

592
00:48:35.109 --> 00:48:39.036
<v LEMELLE>The same thing that’s going on now with Sasol.

593
00:48:39.036 --> 00:48:43.836
Except Sasol is being a little bit more, to me, lenient.

594
00:48:43.836 --> 00:48:49.073
Giving you a different opportunity than what they did in the past.

595
00:48:49.073 --> 00:48:53.000
<v CRAMER>So what did they do in the past?

596
00:48:53.000 --> 00:48:56.235
<v LEMELLE>I'm going to tell you what really happened.

597
00:48:56.235 --> 00:49:03.515
I'm too old to go to jail, so if they sue me for telling the truth, oh well.

598
00:49:03.515 --> 00:49:13.625
What happened to a lot of people when Conoco got ready to purchase land from them, they offered them X amount of dollars to move.

599
00:49:13.625 --> 00:49:19.287
The way it was determined what you were worth was through the tax office.

600
00:49:19.287 --> 00:49:24.140
One hundred and ten percent of people live here was tax exempt,

601
00:49:24.140 --> 00:49:31.015
which meant your property was only worth X amount of dollars, well below what it was worth.

602
00:49:31.015 --> 00:49:33.441
So this is what they offered.

603
00:49:33.441 --> 00:49:42.743
Even the last community, Bel Air, they had people back there had a hundred and something thousand dollar homes but they were . . . Because,

604
00:49:42.743 --> 00:49:49.213
say, seventy-five dollars . . . if you had anything over seventy-five thousand dollars, you had to pay tax.

605
00:49:49.213 --> 00:49:53.662
So to keep from paying tax you signed up . . . you’re exempt.

606
00:49:53.662 --> 00:50:00.537
So the exemption says that if you exempt whatever your property has to be less than seventy-five.

607
00:50:00.537 --> 00:50:04.176
So this is what we going to offer you.

608
00:50:04.176 --> 00:50:10.243
We going to offer you fifty-five thousand dollars, you can take it or leave it.

609
00:50:10.243 --> 00:50:11.456
We coming anyway.

610
00:50:11.456 --> 00:50:13.882
And they would begin to expand.

611
00:50:13.882 --> 00:50:17.118
One person move, well if a person move,

612
00:50:17.118 --> 00:50:24.801
the first thing that company would do was go fence their property and start doing something crazy on it.

613
00:50:24.801 --> 00:50:26.419
So the neighbors say,

614
00:50:26.419 --> 00:50:34.103
"Well if they gone . . . " I think Mr. Ernest Smith was one of the first people to leave Bel Air.

615
00:50:34.103 --> 00:50:39.765
And once he left, the neighbors say, "Well it's time for us to go."

616
00:50:39.765 --> 00:50:40.574
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Ernest Smith?

617
00:50:40.574 --> 00:50:40.978
<v LEMELLE>[Pardon]?

618
00:50:40.978 --> 00:50:41.787
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Ernest Smith.

619
00:50:41.787 --> 00:50:42.191
<v LEMELLE>Thibodaux.

620
00:50:42.191 --> 00:50:43.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Thibodaux.

621
00:50:43.000 --> 00:50:46.965
<v CRAMER>So people started leaving Mossville a while ago?

622
00:50:46.965 --> 00:50:52.417
<v LEMELLE>We had a community of, I say, twelve, fifteen years ago.

623
00:50:52.417 --> 00:50:56.878
Bel Air was . . . we call it Bel Air Subdivision.

624
00:50:56.878 --> 00:51:04.809
I would say that to make up Mossville it was well over three quarters of Mossville.

625
00:51:04.809 --> 00:51:11.252
When we lost that little subdivision, we lost three quarters of our community.

626
00:51:11.252 --> 00:51:14.722
<v CRAMER>So it was about 2000 . . . year 2000?

627
00:51:14.722 --> 00:51:16.704
Right around in there.

628
00:51:16.704 --> 00:51:17.200
<v LEMELLE>Yeah.

629
00:51:17.200 --> 00:51:19.183
Somewhere in that neighborhood.

630
00:51:19.183 --> 00:51:21.165
<v CRAMER>So where'd everybody go?

631
00:51:21.165 --> 00:51:22.652
Just around about?

632
00:51:22.652 --> 00:51:25.130
<v LEMELLE>Just around about Lake Charles.

633
00:51:25.130 --> 00:51:27.609
Some moved to Lake Charles.

634
00:51:27.609 --> 00:51:29.096
Some moved out

635
00:51:29.096 --> 00:51:35.539
<c.p>. . . What's the name of that place out where Brown and them is at?

636
00:51:35.539 --> 00:51:38.513
Your friend, Cynthia Brown and them?

637
00:51:38.513 --> 00:51:39.009
Manchester?

638
00:51:39.009 --> 00:51:40.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Manchester.

639
00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:43.168
<v LEMELLE>But it's all local Lake Charles area.

640
00:51:43.168 --> 00:51:46.337
And some probably went west to Sulphur.

641
00:51:46.337 --> 00:51:46.789
Carlyss.

642
00:51:46.789 --> 00:51:51.316
But majority, I would say, moved to Lake Charles area.

643
00:51:51.316 --> 00:51:59.011
<v CRAMER>So I'm going to take you back to 1946 when you were born . . . right around that time.

644
00:51:59.011 --> 00:51:59.463
<v LEMELLE>Okay.

645
00:51:59.463 --> 00:52:08.516
<v CRAMER>Were any . . . Who did you know that was in the military who served in World War II in your community?

646
00:52:08.516 --> 00:52:13.495
Like when you were growing up, that you heard stories about.

647
00:52:13.495 --> 00:52:15.758
<v LEMELLE>They're still living or deceased?

648
00:52:15.758 --> 00:52:23.000
<v CRAMER>No, that you just . . . Whether it was your father, your uncle, or neighbors, or relatives.

649
00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:28.907
<v LEMELLE>Well I have Mr. Morris Prater, which is my first cousin.

650
00:52:28.907 --> 00:52:31.593
He was in the army.

651
00:52:31.593 --> 00:52:39.111
I'll never forget certain times of the month, whenever, when Grandmother would call for

652
00:52:39.111 --> 00:52:41.796
<c.p>prayer for the service men.

653
00:52:41.796 --> 00:52:45.556
We all prayed for our service men.

654
00:52:45.556 --> 00:52:53.074
And I had . . . my grandmother had a son, by the name of George Towner.

655
00:52:53.074 --> 00:52:57.907
He was in World War, but he's deceased now.

656
00:52:57.907 --> 00:53:00.593
And we used to pray.

657
00:53:00.593 --> 00:53:12.944
That was just part of my grandmother's thing that you prayed for . . . So in my family I know there was Mr. Morris Prater.

658
00:53:12.944 --> 00:53:18.315
My Uncle George Frederick Towner, he was a service man.

659
00:53:18.315 --> 00:53:23.685
You’re putting me on the spot because I can't think.

660
00:53:23.685 --> 00:53:30.667
Kenny Lee's father, back in those days when he was a service man.

661
00:53:30.667 --> 00:53:35.500
My sister that's deceased, her husband was a serviceman.

662
00:53:35.500 --> 00:53:38.185
His name was Alvin Simmons.

663
00:53:38.185 --> 00:53:46.778
All these was the World War II vets that I can remember back in the '40s.

664
00:53:46.778 --> 00:53:50.000
So that's four I know.

665
00:53:50.000 --> 00:53:52.323
And my dad had a brother.

666
00:53:52.323 --> 00:53:54.645
He was an air force man.

667
00:53:54.645 --> 00:54:03.161
He had just got in when he had to go off to war, but they ended up coming back to Lake Charles.

668
00:54:03.161 --> 00:54:07.419
He didn't get a chance to go because he was young.

669
00:54:07.419 --> 00:54:14.774
And he ended up at the Chennault Air Force . . . Chennault Air Force Base, and he used to come here.

670
00:54:14.774 --> 00:54:17.871
And eventually Uncle Johnny Lemelle, he moved here.

671
00:54:17.871 --> 00:54:21.355
Who else I know who went in the service?

672
00:54:21.355 --> 00:54:24.065
Oh, my sister Julia's husband, Dewey Gordwin.

673
00:54:24.065 --> 00:54:27.548
He was a World War II vet from here.

674
00:54:27.548 --> 00:54:31.419
So in my family . . . I can't think of anybody else.

675
00:54:31.419 --> 00:54:35.677
The more I think about and see their pictures I remember.

676
00:54:35.677 --> 00:54:42.645
<v CRAMER>So it's a number of men who served in World War II who came back to the community.

677
00:54:42.645 --> 00:54:43.032
<v LEMELLE>Right.

678
00:54:43.032 --> 00:54:50.387
<v CRAMER>How did that affect . . . How did their service and their experience in the war . . . Did people talk about it?

679
00:54:50.387 --> 00:54:55.032
Did they come back and have some civic leadership aspects to their . . .

680
00:54:55.032 --> 00:55:02.000
<c.p>I guess I'm just curious about how their service and their involvement in the war changed things.

681
00:55:02.000 --> 00:55:11.190
<v LEMELLE>I think really, Jennifer, it was the same that you hear now that you heard from veterans that came from Vietnam War.

682
00:55:11.190 --> 00:55:14.532
You go off and fight for your country,

683
00:55:14.532 --> 00:55:25.392
and you come back and you can't even get a decent job in the community or an area like Lake Charles, as large as we are.

684
00:55:25.392 --> 00:55:29.570
How many veterans have to travel from here to Houston?

685
00:55:29.570 --> 00:55:35.000
Morris Prater goes to Houston probably twice . . . once or twice a month.

686
00:55:35.000 --> 00:55:36.944
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Some go up to Shreveport.

687
00:55:36.944 --> 00:55:38.889
<v LEMELLE>Some go to Alexandria [Louisiana].

688
00:55:38.889 --> 00:55:40.444
Some goes to Shreveport.

689
00:55:40.444 --> 00:55:45.889
But what I'm saying, like my Uncle Frederick . . . I can remember them saying . . . like,

690
00:55:45.889 --> 00:55:50.944
they used some words that we don't like to use, "Dammit to hell.

691
00:55:50.944 --> 00:56:01.056
I been off fighting in the war; I come home, y'all don't even let me go to the front gate of Cities Service because I'm black.

692
00:56:01.056 --> 00:56:08.444
But it was okay for me to go over there and get on the front line and take bullets."

693
00:56:08.444 --> 00:56:13.500
It's the same thing that you heard then is what you hear now.

694
00:56:13.500 --> 00:56:15.444
A veteran got no privileges.

695
00:56:15.444 --> 00:56:25.167
Well you couldn't even get a decent job so what we do, what we did is we go on what we learn from our mother.

696
00:56:25.167 --> 00:56:29.444
My uncles were some of the greatest carpenters in the world.

697
00:56:29.444 --> 00:56:34.500
So Fredrick came on back from the war and he took up carpentry.

698
00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:38.000
Got with his brothers and they did carpentry work.

699
00:56:38.000 --> 00:56:39.944
That's all he ever did,

700
00:56:39.944 --> 00:56:59.000
but it's the first to say that he should have been able to go with his brother-in-law Earthy Prater and have a job that paid at least three dollars an hour instead of having to hammer nails until his hands is like that because he was a veteran.

701
00:56:59.000 --> 00:57:03.430
So you heard the same thing then that you hear now.

702
00:57:03.430 --> 00:57:05.846
People went off to the war,

703
00:57:05.846 --> 00:57:12.693
<c.p>went on the front line and come back to the community and you find the same thing.

704
00:57:12.693 --> 00:57:17.928
You come back from the war that you fought for somebody else's rights,

705
00:57:17.928 --> 00:57:27.191
and you come home and your children or your nieces and nephews is going to a little small school with ten year-old books.

706
00:57:27.191 --> 00:57:29.205
And it was really frustrating.

707
00:57:29.205 --> 00:57:32.024
I tell you, that part I know.

708
00:57:32.024 --> 00:57:40.884
Because in '46, and after '46 the years passed, I ended up . . . Eventually I had to get up and go to school.

709
00:57:40.884 --> 00:57:43.300
But it was the same then.

710
00:57:43.300 --> 00:57:51.758
Most people that . . . men that went off to the war that came back home that came back with a different attitude,

711
00:57:51.758 --> 00:57:53.771
but with the same results:

712
00:57:53.771 --> 00:58:02.631
“I know a lot more than I did three years ago when I was a kid going off into Uncle Sam's army.

713
00:58:02.631 --> 00:58:04.242
I'm eighteen years old.

714
00:58:04.242 --> 00:58:05.451
I come back.

715
00:58:05.451 --> 00:58:13.505
I'm twenty-three years old and the same thing I'm getting now is what I got when I was a kid.

716
00:58:13.505 --> 00:58:15.922
You're not giving me anything different.”

717
00:58:15.922 --> 00:58:20.754
My Uncle Fredrick, he was actually . . . I'm going to call it anger.

718
00:58:20.754 --> 00:58:28.003
Sometimes . . . And you see the same thing that’s going on with people that's been in Vietnam or whatever.

719
00:58:28.003 --> 00:58:31.628
They come home with some anger or some hurt.

720
00:58:31.628 --> 00:58:36.058
And he always was one that . . . He always talked about it.

721
00:58:36.058 --> 00:58:41.294
He used to tell us, "If you can, don't go in the army.

722
00:58:41.294 --> 00:58:44.515
If you can, stay out of the army.

723
00:58:44.515 --> 00:58:47.737
You don't need to go to the army.

724
00:58:47.737 --> 00:58:52.570
If you can make it without going in the army, don't go.

725
00:58:52.570 --> 00:58:57.000
Because all they did is made it bad for me."

726
00:58:57.000 --> 00:59:00.717
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Yeah, remember Murphy used to write us some letters.

727
00:59:00.717 --> 00:59:01.130
<v LEMELLE>Yeah.

728
00:59:01.130 --> 00:59:06.087
<v LEONA LEMELLE>And Kenny used to tell us, too, about the army . . . or war.

729
00:59:06.087 --> 00:59:06.500
<v LEMELLE>Yeah.

730
00:59:06.500 --> 00:59:16.826
So I'm saying the same stories I heard then, I turn around and I hear it 1945 to 1970, '71, he heard the same stories.

731
00:59:16.826 --> 00:59:20.543
Kenny Lee went off, and he's sick now . . . Vietnam.

732
00:59:20.543 --> 00:59:27.565
I had another friend who used to write us all the time, we cried and prayed for.

733
00:59:27.565 --> 00:59:31.283
He was a classmate, and he come back home.

734
00:59:31.283 --> 00:59:32.109
Things change.

735
00:59:32.109 --> 00:59:35.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>They got [delivered?] was what happened.

736
00:59:35.000 --> 00:59:37.327
<v LEMELLE>Things change over the years.

737
00:59:37.327 --> 00:59:41.982
Now the younger guys, they come back with this attitude,

738
00:59:41.982 --> 00:59:51.291
but through changes they have a better opportunity than the man did, the veteran did, twenty years prior to him.

739
00:59:51.291 --> 00:59:55.945
Forty, fifty, sixty, seventy, thirty years later things have changed.

740
00:59:55.945 --> 01:00:07.582
So you have an opportunity now because of the blood-sweat that your ancestors went through that you can go up to Cities Service and say,

741
01:00:07.582 --> 01:00:11.305
"Okay, I'm coming to apply for a job.

742
01:00:11.305 --> 01:00:13.167
This is my resume.

743
01:00:13.167 --> 01:00:15.495
I've been to the service.

744
01:00:15.495 --> 01:00:16.891
I'm a veteran.

745
01:00:16.891 --> 01:00:19.218
I could pass this test."

746
01:00:19.218 --> 01:00:30.389
But a lot of our people's held back not form their skills, because they always had great minds, but this is what killed them.

747
01:00:30.389 --> 01:00:35.975
When you sit down . . . I had a teacher, she was Ms. P.M.

748
01:00:35.975 --> 01:00:51.800
Washington, and 1960 she became as our interim principal because the principal that was there messed up so bad and that she was the teacher that taught here when there was a school here.

749
01:00:51.800 --> 01:00:55.989
She told us, and we laughed, she got up,

750
01:00:55.989 --> 01:01:04.833
called the school into the auditorium for her to talk to her kids three days after she became principal.

751
01:01:04.833 --> 01:01:16.004
And she said, "I want to tell all of my students that I love you, and I really want you to take and learn.

752
01:01:16.004 --> 01:01:23.916
We have teachers here that's going to give to you freely, not going to cost you anything.

753
01:01:23.916 --> 01:01:30.433
The only thing it cost you is paying attention, and putting forth an effort."

754
01:01:30.433 --> 01:01:33.225
She said that, and we laughed.

755
01:01:33.225 --> 01:01:41.604
She said, “There’s going to come a time that you going to need an education to wash dishes."

756
01:01:41.604 --> 01:01:43.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>That's right.

757
01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:47.621
<v LEMELLE>You going to need an education to put in an application.

758
01:01:47.621 --> 01:01:51.401
You going to get an education to wash dishes?

759
01:01:51.401 --> 01:01:53.082
Anybody can wash dishes.

760
01:01:53.082 --> 01:02:01.483
But at this day and time, people want a job at Walmart but they can even put in an application.

761
01:02:01.483 --> 01:02:09.885
You go to a big, big restaurant, you apply and say, "Well I don't have it, but I'm a dishwasher."

762
01:02:09.885 --> 01:02:11.145
Can you read?

763
01:02:11.145 --> 01:02:14.926
You can’t read how to even operate the dishwasher.

764
01:02:14.926 --> 01:02:21.647
So the things that she told us was so true, but we couldn’t see it then.

765
01:02:21.647 --> 01:02:28.788
That was one of these stories that some things happen in your life, you just never forget.

766
01:02:28.788 --> 01:02:38.870
Like I keep saying, my Uncle Frederick, he had a lot of anger in him, and a lot of it was from the war.

767
01:02:38.870 --> 01:02:43.071
<v CRAMER>Where did he serve when he was in the war?

768
01:02:43.071 --> 01:02:49.792
<v LEMELLE>Wherever it was at because he did one term somewhere overseas, and he came back . . . cut.

769
01:02:49.792 --> 01:02:55.253
And to keep from going back, he beat up one of his sergeants.

770
01:02:55.253 --> 01:03:03.234
It was like the day he found out they were supposed to be shipped off going back overseas somewhere.

771
01:03:03.234 --> 01:03:12.056
So what he did, he got up in the middle of the evening and had a brawl-out fight with his sergeant,

772
01:03:12.056 --> 01:03:14.996
or whoever was in command of him.

773
01:03:14.996 --> 01:03:23.398
And with this, they say he beat this man up so bad they put him in what they call stockade.

774
01:03:23.398 --> 01:03:27.178
They put him in stockade for so many days.

775
01:03:27.178 --> 01:03:36.000
He said it was the happiest day of his life because he didn't have to board and go back overseas.

776
01:03:36.000 --> 01:03:37.600
<v CRAMER>And that was during World War II, right?

777
01:03:37.600 --> 01:03:38.600
<v LEMELLE>That was World War II.

778
01:03:38.600 --> 01:03:39.400
<v CRAMER>So he came back?

779
01:03:39.400 --> 01:03:42.000
Did he come back because of an injury, or did they just . . .?

780
01:03:42.000 --> 01:03:43.577
<v LEMELLE>No, he just got discharged.

781
01:03:43.577 --> 01:03:48.307
He came home, but he came home with a lot a lot of animosity, anger.

782
01:03:48.307 --> 01:03:53.353
Anger and hurt can . . . you don't know which way to take it if you just hurt . . .

783
01:03:53.353 --> 01:03:53.668
<v CRAMER>Yeah.

784
01:03:53.668 --> 01:03:56.191
<v LEMELLE>. . . or if you have a lot of anger.

785
01:03:56.191 --> 01:03:57.768
It was a thin line.

786
01:03:57.768 --> 01:04:04.075
And another thing that hurt a lot of the veterans then, they didn't have any place for them to go.

787
01:04:04.075 --> 01:04:04.390
<v CRAMER>Right.

788
01:04:04.390 --> 01:04:08.174
<v LEMELLE>Where do you go and who do you tell your problems to?

789
01:04:08.174 --> 01:04:10.697
Well I don't have anyone to tell them.

790
01:04:10.697 --> 01:04:12.589
Take it out on your wife.

791
01:04:12.589 --> 01:04:14.481
Take your anger out on somebody.

792
01:04:14.481 --> 01:04:15.427
Take it out.

793
01:04:15.427 --> 01:04:21.734
So he said a lot of times what he take his anger out on was a hammer and a nail.

794
01:04:21.734 --> 01:04:26.780
He said, "I can beat this nail and it ain't going to do me nothing back.

795
01:04:26.780 --> 01:04:28.988
Nobody can't do me nothing about it.”

796
01:04:28.988 --> 01:04:31.195
I said, "I'm going to fetch it."

797
01:04:31.195 --> 01:04:36.241
And he . . . Poor thing, when he died, from holding a hammer, his hand was like that.

798
01:04:36.241 --> 01:04:46.017
But he said, "I could take this hammer and a nail and I could beat it on anything I can beat it and it's not going to cause me no problems."

799
01:04:46.017 --> 01:04:51.062
<v CRAMER>So he was angry, I'm guessing, at how he was being treated, the lack of opportunity.

800
01:04:51.062 --> 01:04:56.739
I mean, because there was rampant, at that time in the late '40s or early '50s, with fighting . . .

801
01:04:56.739 --> 01:04:58.000
<v LEMELLE>It was bad.

802
01:04:58.000 --> 01:05:01.913
<v CRAMER>. . . with Jim Crow laws and that sort of thing.

803
01:05:01.913 --> 01:05:04.522
That's what he was angry about?

804
01:05:04.522 --> 01:05:11.043
<v LEONA LEMELLE>When Uncle Ferdinand, he went to that war and he came back fighting and cussing.

805
01:05:11.043 --> 01:05:18.870
<v LEMELLE>She had an uncle like that, and he just had a lot of anger and hurt and anger.

806
01:05:18.870 --> 01:05:21.478
<v LEONA LEMELLE>And was so intelligent [up here?].

807
01:05:21.478 --> 01:05:24.522
<v CRAMER>So what do people do about it?

808
01:05:24.522 --> 01:05:31.913
I mean, were there any civil rights activists in the community who were fighting for anything or . . .?

809
01:05:31.913 --> 01:05:35.391
<v LEMELLE>Not in that time that I know of.

810
01:05:35.391 --> 01:05:42.348
In a community like this, we had leaders that . . . It was one thing I can say,

811
01:05:42.348 --> 01:05:46.261
Mossville at one time we were always one-hundred percent.

812
01:05:46.261 --> 01:05:57.565
Whatever was going on as far as elections, who you needed in office, the elders would get together and they would all be on one accord.

813
01:05:57.565 --> 01:06:03.652
There was a man here by the name of . . . I don't know if you

814
01:06:03.652 --> 01:06:05.391
<c.p>met Della Dotson yet.

815
01:06:05.391 --> 01:06:08.000
<v CRAMER>I think I interviewed Della.

816
01:06:08.000 --> 01:06:14.453
<v LEMELLE>Her father was . . . I'm going to call him a leader here in the community.

817
01:06:14.453 --> 01:06:24.133
And what they would do, these men would get together and discuss who was to be your sheriff for Calcasieu Parish,

818
01:06:24.133 --> 01:06:28.281
who was to be this, that for the parish.

819
01:06:28.281 --> 01:06:33.352
And Mount Zion and different churches they would all get together.

820
01:06:33.352 --> 01:06:42.109
The men would get together, and most of the time what it was, was at Mr. Josh Rigmaiden's place.

821
01:06:42.109 --> 01:06:44.875
And we called him the mayor.

822
01:06:44.875 --> 01:06:45.336
<v CRAMER>Okay.

823
01:06:45.336 --> 01:06:48.102
<v LEMELLE>That was the mayor of Mossville.

824
01:06:48.102 --> 01:06:52.711
But at that time . . . I showed you that picture, baby.

825
01:06:52.711 --> 01:06:53.633
<v LEONA LEMELLE>This it?

826
01:06:53.633 --> 01:06:56.859
<v REBECCA COOPER>Can I take a picture of it?

827
01:06:56.859 --> 01:06:58.242
<v LEMELLE>If you can.

828
01:06:58.242 --> 01:07:07.000
It's kind of . . . When we went to see your friend Lola I got this picture off from hers.

829
01:07:07.000 --> 01:07:09.143
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Oh yeah, at her house.

830
01:07:09.143 --> 01:07:10.429
I remember now.

831
01:07:10.429 --> 01:07:13.857
<v LEMELLE>And for some reason the light hit it.

832
01:07:13.857 --> 01:07:16.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>That was Paw-Paw, huh?

833
01:07:16.000 --> 01:07:19.068
<v LEMELLE>It was her daddy, Lola's daddy.

834
01:07:19.068 --> 01:07:20.091
Lola's father.

835
01:07:20.091 --> 01:07:23.159
Want me to hold it up?

836
01:07:23.159 --> 01:07:28.784
This was our mayor, what we considered the mayor of Mossville.

837
01:07:28.784 --> 01:07:38.500
A lot of things that we did accomplish in the community was because of this man and other leaders.

838
01:07:38.500 --> 01:07:46.170
So eventually, eventually, eventually as time passed, you eventually got things that you really needed.

839
01:07:46.170 --> 01:07:56.909
I can’t say about men that went to the war or what happened to them, if it ever changed for them.

840
01:07:56.909 --> 01:08:02.023
Some got lucky, some got a break, and some didn't.

841
01:08:02.023 --> 01:08:12.250
Like my sister Julia, her husband end up going . . . he was a veteran but end up working for Cities Service.

842
01:08:12.250 --> 01:08:14.295
Uncle Frederick never did.

843
01:08:14.295 --> 01:08:18.898
But Morris Prater ended up working . . . Where did Morris . . . ?

844
01:08:18.898 --> 01:08:22.477
He worked at one of the plants.

845
01:08:22.477 --> 01:08:26.568
So some of them got a good break,

846
01:08:26.568 --> 01:08:37.818
but it was through the efforts of people like Josh Rigmaiden that talked . . . Back then, it was a family thing, you know.

847
01:08:37.818 --> 01:08:43.443
If you got lucky and got a break, not just veterans,

848
01:08:43.443 --> 01:08:50.602
but . . . I'm going to use the name Mr. Audrey Prater, an old resident here.

849
01:08:50.602 --> 01:08:53.670
He was the cousin of Earthy.

850
01:08:53.670 --> 01:08:59.295
Well, Audrey went to work at Conoco as a young man.

851
01:08:59.295 --> 01:09:09.523
And with him being working at Conoco he had other friends that came to the community such as Valery Montgomery.

852
01:09:09.523 --> 01:09:13.614
He would say, "Well I got a friend."

853
01:09:13.614 --> 01:09:21.284
Mr. Josh Rigmaiden would go and say, "I got a young man here in Mossville.

854
01:09:21.284 --> 01:09:25.886
He need a job because he got a family.

855
01:09:25.886 --> 01:09:27.932
He got four kids.

856
01:09:27.932 --> 01:09:31.000
Y'all try to help him."

857
01:09:31.000 --> 01:09:37.408
So eventually . . . And the old saying, “You scratch my back I'll scratch yours.”

858
01:09:37.408 --> 01:09:46.775
But a lot of people won't admit a lot of things happened simply because of leadership that you followed.

859
01:09:46.775 --> 01:09:52.690
When politicians saw that you were on one accord, that was power.

860
01:09:52.690 --> 01:09:53.676
You had

861
01:09:53.676 --> 01:09:56.634
<c.p>power when you were all together.

862
01:09:56.634 --> 01:10:03.535
And they used to tell us if we stick together . . . They used to demonstrate.

863
01:10:03.535 --> 01:10:11.915
At present time, my poor little hands . . . I can’t think of carrying this because it's all together.

864
01:10:11.915 --> 01:10:17.338
But if I take a sheet at a time, I can.

865
01:10:17.338 --> 01:10:19.310
So they believed then,

866
01:10:19.310 --> 01:10:30.648
and that's one thing we lost in this community that probably wouldn't have happened to a lot of people, because we lost leadership.

867
01:10:30.648 --> 01:10:39.521
Things wouldn’t have happened if we had people that had voice to go up and talk for you.

868
01:10:39.521 --> 01:10:41.000
<v CRAMER>And negotiate.

869
01:10:41.000 --> 01:10:41.442
<v LEMELLE>Negotiate.

870
01:10:41.442 --> 01:10:46.309
<v CRAMER>And probably could offer block voting and that sort of thing.

871
01:10:46.309 --> 01:10:48.078
<v LEMELLE>They had . . . I'm serious.

872
01:10:48.078 --> 01:10:55.156
If you were a kid here, a young man or woman or whoever from this community,

873
01:10:55.156 --> 01:10:59.138
and something happened . . . if you got in a wreck,

874
01:10:59.138 --> 01:11:05.773
got in a brawl, or got whatever, got into trouble that they had to say,

875
01:11:05.773 --> 01:11:11.967
"We going to arrest you and bring you to jail," you called Josh Rigmaiden.

876
01:11:11.967 --> 01:11:17.717
Josh Rigmaiden would call Ham Reed and say, "That's one of my boys.

877
01:11:17.717 --> 01:11:22.584
Don't you lock him up no further than where he's at.

878
01:11:22.584 --> 01:11:25.238
I'll be there to get him."

879
01:11:25.238 --> 01:11:27.450
That was a done deal.

880
01:11:27.450 --> 01:11:28.777
<v CRAMER>Who's Ham Reed?

881
01:11:28.777 --> 01:11:33.643
<v LEMELLE>He used to be the . . . That's right, you not from here.

882
01:11:33.643 --> 01:11:35.855
Ham Reed was the sheriff

883
01:11:35.855 --> 01:11:38.067
<c.p>of Calcasieu Parish for years.

884
01:11:38.067 --> 01:11:46.030
And Mr. Frank Salter was at that time back in the day was the . . . What was Frank Salter?

885
01:11:46.030 --> 01:11:46.914
District attorney.

886
01:11:46.914 --> 01:11:49.126
But people respected Josh Rigmaiden.

887
01:11:49.126 --> 01:11:53.993
If Josh Rigmaiden said, "You have Mr. Lemelle's son up there?"

888
01:11:53.993 --> 01:11:56.647
Just using it as an example.

889
01:11:56.647 --> 01:11:57.089
"Yeah."

890
01:11:57.089 --> 01:11:59.743
"This is Josh Rigmaiden from Mossville.

891
01:11:59.743 --> 01:12:01.955
Be there in a minute.

892
01:12:01.955 --> 01:12:03.725
Don't lock him up."

893
01:12:03.725 --> 01:12:04.610
"Yes, sir."

894
01:12:04.610 --> 01:12:08.149
And they saw a community that was together.

895
01:12:08.149 --> 01:12:09.476
Simple as that.

896
01:12:09.476 --> 01:12:10.803
And everybody went.

897
01:12:10.803 --> 01:12:13.015
They would talk it over.

898
01:12:13.015 --> 01:12:16.112
Who's going to be your next sheriff?

899
01:12:16.112 --> 01:12:19.208
Who's going to be the district attorney?

900
01:12:19.208 --> 01:12:28.498
And at that time you had, in the parish, if you had extra 2,000 votes, that was a lot for you.

901
01:12:28.498 --> 01:12:32.922
Five hundred votes, man I want those five hundred votes.

902
01:12:32.922 --> 01:12:33.364
<v CRAMER>Yep.

903
01:12:33.364 --> 01:12:38.230
<v LEMELLE>So that's the way I can remember things for the community.

904
01:12:38.230 --> 01:12:40.000
<v CRAMER>Voting is power.

905
01:12:40.000 --> 01:12:41.902
<v LEMELLE>Voting is power.

906
01:12:41.902 --> 01:12:53.951
<v CRAMER>So Josh Rigmaiden, after he was gone, who kind of replaced him as the de facto mayor of Mossville?

907
01:12:53.951 --> 01:13:02.195
<v LEMELLE>Well, we had other leaders, but I wouldn't say they were as effective.

908
01:13:02.195 --> 01:13:06.000
<v CRAMER>What made him so effective?

909
01:13:06.000 --> 01:13:10.615
<v LEMELLE>What made him so effective?

910
01:13:10.615 --> 01:13:11.538
Unity.

911
01:13:11.538 --> 01:13:18.000
<v CRAMER>And his ability to get everybody . . .?

912
01:13:18.000 --> 01:13:24.011
<v LEMELLE>His ability to get everybody together and the unity.

913
01:13:24.011 --> 01:13:38.038
And I don't know, I'm just saying they didn't fear him, but he was a man that had the right words.

914
01:13:38.038 --> 01:13:44.718
He had a way of having people to follow him.

915
01:13:44.718 --> 01:13:46.721
He was business.

916
01:13:46.721 --> 01:13:56.073
He had a little business, a little store and this, that and the other.

917
01:13:56.073 --> 01:14:01.416
Mr. Josh was, to us, a powerful man.

918
01:14:01.416 --> 01:14:11.435
He had a nephew by the name of James Rigmaiden who, in the later years,

919
01:14:11.435 --> 01:14:23.458
became an adult, and he was a voice but he didn't have the unity that Josh Rigmaiden had.

920
01:14:23.458 --> 01:14:26.798
He had a great voice,

921
01:14:26.798 --> 01:14:46.836
but of course he no longer had a Ham Reed and he no longer had a Frank Salter during the time they had a big thing with the Jupiter Plant.

922
01:14:46.836 --> 01:14:53.515
A lot of things happened with unions . . . changeover in government.

923
01:14:53.515 --> 01:15:06.206
So this made Mossville not as effective as they were in the '40s and '50s . . . or in the '50s,

924
01:15:06.206 --> 01:15:17.561
so somewhere in the early '60s it began to change because of government change was one thing.

925
01:15:17.561 --> 01:15:31.588
Later on as we began to see the schools, they no longer . . . Trying to find the word to keep from hurting.

926
01:15:31.588 --> 01:15:44.279
We no longer had followers that was so . . . Use the word . . . I don't want to use the wrong word.

927
01:15:44.279 --> 01:15:50.290
But some things you can’t get from being radical.

928
01:15:50.290 --> 01:15:54.298
I'm going to use that word.

929
01:15:54.298 --> 01:16:10.328
You had more people that wanted to just take things over in a radical way than a way of words and wisdom and understanding.

930
01:16:10.328 --> 01:16:13.000
<v CRAMER>Like a gentle . . .

931
01:16:13.000 --> 01:16:13.841
<v LEMELLE>A gentleness.

932
01:16:13.841 --> 01:16:18.045
Even the Bible speak that a soft answer turns away

933
01:16:18.045 --> 01:16:18.465
<c.p>wrath.

934
01:16:18.465 --> 01:16:26.873
So some things you didn't accomplish because you had the attitude that I'm the man and you’re going to . . . No,

935
01:16:26.873 --> 01:16:28.975
it doesn't work that way.

936
01:16:28.975 --> 01:16:38.223
If my wife and I get into it, just because I'm the male factor that don't mean I can just buffalo her.

937
01:16:38.223 --> 01:16:39.064
You know?

938
01:16:39.064 --> 01:16:43.268
This is the way . . . You have a change in government,

939
01:16:43.268 --> 01:16:49.153
you have a change in people’s attitude, and your attitude is a big thing.

940
01:16:49.153 --> 01:16:53.357
Josh Rigmaiden was a friend of people in higher places.

941
01:16:53.357 --> 01:16:57.981
And what made him effective because when they asked the question,

942
01:16:57.981 --> 01:17:04.707
"I got nine men out here and they all is on the same page with me.

943
01:17:04.707 --> 01:17:14.376
I got 300 families out here, and they all saying that this is what we going to do, this is what we want."

944
01:17:14.376 --> 01:17:16.898
So it was a lot easier.

945
01:17:16.898 --> 01:17:19.000
But people, people change.

946
01:17:19.000 --> 01:17:22.292
Younger generations are always more radical.

947
01:17:22.292 --> 01:17:34.912
More just want to knock the door down, and all you got to do is wait till they unlock it and open easy.

948
01:17:34.912 --> 01:17:38.204
That's the generation I came through.

949
01:17:38.204 --> 01:17:47.532
I never was a . . . I was a thug, but I wasn't radical when it came to it.

950
01:17:47.532 --> 01:17:53.568
When I didn't go through the . . . I was before the integration,

951
01:17:53.568 --> 01:18:08.931
but I always had doors open for me because when I was younger . . . from the eighth grade I lived here and I went to live in Boulder, Colorado.

952
01:18:08.931 --> 01:18:18.259
And that was like leaving a dark room, going into one that is wide open of light.

953
01:18:18.259 --> 01:18:29.233
I came from a small community, one hundred percent black, and an all-black school from first grade to eighth grade.

954
01:18:29.233 --> 01:18:33.622
I left here and went to Boulder, Colorado.

955
01:18:33.622 --> 01:18:39.658
My freshman year at Boulder High School was over 1,500 freshmen.

956
01:18:39.658 --> 01:18:46.242
Out of the entire freshman class there was four African American students.

957
01:18:46.242 --> 01:18:56.119
Can you imagine the change for a fifteen year-old kid that was totally surrounded by parents’ love, attention?

958
01:18:56.119 --> 01:19:00.508
I was totally like, "Man, where am I?

959
01:19:00.508 --> 01:19:03.252
What am I doing here?"

960
01:19:03.252 --> 01:19:12.580
"Well it's because we want you to go and this is going to be a better opportunity.

961
01:19:12.580 --> 01:19:16.420
You going to get a better education."

962
01:19:16.420 --> 01:19:24.651
How can I get a better . . . All these things going through a fifteen year-old's mind.

963
01:19:24.651 --> 01:19:33.430
But it was like a total eclipse from me going from first grade through eighth grade.

964
01:19:33.430 --> 01:19:38.917
I looked to my left, there was my friend African

965
01:19:38.917 --> 01:19:39.466
<c.p>American.

966
01:19:39.466 --> 01:19:47.696
I looked to my right, I looked up there my instructor was an African American.

967
01:19:47.696 --> 01:19:53.183
I look all around me, that's all I ever saw.

968
01:19:53.183 --> 01:20:03.059
I go home in the afternoon, I saw my black mother, and my black father, my black sister,

969
01:20:03.059 --> 01:20:11.838
and my black nieces and nephews and here I'm looking like, what am I doing here.

970
01:20:11.838 --> 01:20:12.936
My God.

971
01:20:12.936 --> 01:20:22.812
This is when I learned that it's not where you are, but it's what you desire to be.

972
01:20:22.812 --> 01:20:29.397
The same thing that I get here I could get at home.

973
01:20:29.397 --> 01:20:32.689
But it’s such a big difference.

974
01:20:32.689 --> 01:20:40.371
This is one thing I learned, and I'm going to teach you something today.

975
01:20:40.371 --> 01:20:50.247
That if you ever had something that held you down, that you never want to go back to,

976
01:20:50.247 --> 01:20:56.831
anything in your life that triggers you to be wrong or whatever,

977
01:20:56.831 --> 01:21:07.257
the only thing that can carry you back to that is three things—what is the definition of a noun?

978
01:21:07.257 --> 01:21:10.000
<v CRAMER>Person, place, or thing.

979
01:21:10.000 --> 01:21:10.429
<v LEMELLE>Okay.

980
01:21:10.429 --> 01:21:13.000
Things that was in my life.

981
01:21:13.000 --> 01:21:18.571
The first thing I learned about a noun: The definition is a person.

982
01:21:18.571 --> 01:21:22.429
That's the wrong person for you to be with.

983
01:21:22.429 --> 01:21:23.714
Okay, the place.

984
01:21:23.714 --> 01:21:27.571
Where did you go to get in that trouble?

985
01:21:27.571 --> 01:21:29.286
Don't go back there.

986
01:21:29.286 --> 01:21:30.143
The thing.

987
01:21:30.143 --> 01:21:34.000
Was it a smoke on a hit of marijuana?

988
01:21:34.000 --> 01:21:36.143
Was it an alcohol bottle?

989
01:21:36.143 --> 01:21:37.857
So I learn that.

990
01:21:37.857 --> 01:21:47.286
A person, place, or thing will cause you to go back to where you were, and you never want to go there.

991
01:21:47.286 --> 01:21:52.857
So if you eliminate the noun out of your life, you got it.

992
01:21:52.857 --> 01:21:55.000
So now Jennifer, you know.

993
01:21:55.000 --> 01:21:57.143
A person, place, or thing.

994
01:21:57.143 --> 01:21:58.857
You okay, my baby?

995
01:21:58.857 --> 01:22:02.714
<v CRAMER>You want some water or a drink or something?

996
01:22:02.714 --> 01:22:04.000
<v LEONA LEMELLE>Cold water.

997
01:22:04.000 --> 01:22:06.824
<v LEMELLE>I think I got some Sprite.

998
01:22:06.824 --> 01:22:09.176
<v LEONA LEMELLE>I don’t want no soda.

999
01:22:09.176 --> 01:22:09.647
<v CRAMER>Okay.

1000
01:22:09.647 --> 01:22:12.941
Becca's going to get you some water.

1001
01:22:12.941 --> 01:22:18.118
I know we're about at . . . almost an hour and a half.

1002
01:22:18.118 --> 01:22:28.471
And you know, of course, this is just part two, and there will be a part three, and probably a part four.

1003
01:22:28.471 --> 01:22:36.000
And I have so many questions left to ask you and so many good things.

1004
01:22:36.000 --> 01:22:37.378
<v LEMELLE>Well ask me quick.

1005
01:22:37.378 --> 01:22:39.789
I won't talk so long next time.

1006
01:22:39.789 --> 01:22:40.823
<v CRAMER>No, it's beautiful.

1007
01:22:40.823 --> 01:22:44.612
It's just there's so much there, and you're a great storyteller.

1008
01:22:44.612 --> 01:22:49.090
So I appreciate, as does anybody who's going to be listening to this,

1009
01:22:49.090 --> 01:22:53.913
all of the information that you're providing that a lot of people don't have.

1010
01:22:53.913 --> 01:22:58.736
There's a perspective that you have that is unique and very informative and insightful.

1011
01:22:58.736 --> 01:23:04.936
So as long as you’ll keep talking to me, I'm going to keep . . . we're going to keep recording.

1012
01:23:04.936 --> 01:23:11.137
So probably what I'll do is wrap it up here, so we can go to your mom's house . . .

1013
01:23:11.137 --> 01:23:11.482
<v LEMELLE>Okay.

1014
01:23:11.482 --> 01:23:12.860
<v CRAMER>. . . and video tape it.

1015
01:23:12.860 --> 01:23:13.204
<v LEMELLE>Alright.

1016
01:23:13.204 --> 01:23:18.371
<v CRAMER>And then when we come back, there's some other things that we can talk about.

1017
01:23:18.371 --> 01:23:23.538
About life . . . general life in Mossville and also some specific points in history as well.

1018
01:23:23.538 --> 01:23:29.395
We've already talked a little bit about it, but what I'll do is I’ll get it transcribed.

1019
01:23:29.395 --> 01:23:38.007
What we've just done, I'll get that transcribed, and I'll bring a copy of this audio back to you and a copy of this transcription.

1020
01:23:38.007 --> 01:23:45.930
And before I come back I'll go through here and see if there's anything I want to tease out a little bit more,

1021
01:23:45.930 --> 01:23:50.064
because you already answered a lot of questions about World War II.

1022
01:23:50.064 --> 01:23:55.920
That’s . . . I always think World War II has a huge impact on a community no matter what.

1023
01:23:55.920 --> 01:23:58.676
And it seems like . . . and people as well.

1024
01:23:58.676 --> 01:24:03.843
And we talked a little bit . . . we talked a lot about that and that's great.

1025
01:24:03.843 --> 01:24:07.977
So next time we'll probably maybe try to get to various neighborhoods.

1026
01:24:07.977 --> 01:24:10.043
<v LEMELLE>I'll tell you something now, Jennifer.

1027
01:24:10.043 --> 01:24:10.388
<v CRAMER>Yes.

1028
01:24:10.388 --> 01:24:17.967
<v LEMELLE>It might be good for you, too, with the generations that you going to talk to along with the World War II . . .

1029
01:24:17.967 --> 01:24:19.000
<v CRAMER>Yes, sir.

1030
01:24:19.000 --> 01:24:19.754
<v LEMELLE>Is Vietnam.

1031
01:24:19.754 --> 01:24:20.507
<v CRAMER>Oh yes.

1032
01:24:20.507 --> 01:24:22.014
Some people won't talk.

1033
01:24:22.014 --> 01:24:31.058
We'll hear from other people, for example, that so-and-so isn't comfortable talking about Vietnam or isn't comfortable talking about Korea or something like that.

1034
01:24:31.058 --> 01:24:34.826
So we don't ever want to make somebody feel uncomfortable.

1035
01:24:34.826 --> 01:24:35.203
<v LEMELLE>Right.

1036
01:24:35.203 --> 01:24:42.362
<v CRAMER>But if somebody has something to share, we're definitely . . . Because you know, World War II is pretty well documented.

1037
01:24:42.362 --> 01:24:44.246
But you know what isn't?

1038
01:24:44.246 --> 01:24:45.000
Korea.

1039
01:24:45.000 --> 01:24:45.383
<v LEMELLE>Korea.

1040
01:24:45.383 --> 01:24:45.766
<v CRAMER>Vietnam.

1041
01:24:45.766 --> 01:24:46.148
<v LEMELLE>Right.

1042
01:24:46.148 --> 01:24:53.039
<v CRAMER>So much of that is just not documented, and we'd love to get some of that on . . . recorded.

1043
01:24:53.039 --> 01:24:59.547
So if you want to encourage some people to open up and kind of talk about that.

1044
01:24:59.547 --> 01:25:03.758
And anything they're not comfortable talking about, we definitely won't broach.

1045
01:25:03.758 --> 01:25:09.500
But even if it's just like, hey, what was it like to be in Korea?

1046
01:25:09.500 --> 01:25:11.031
What was that like.

1047
01:25:11.031 --> 01:25:11.797
And/or Vietnam.

1048
01:25:11.797 --> 01:25:12.945
Were you drafted?

1049
01:25:12.945 --> 01:25:16.008
What branch of service did you belong to?

1050
01:25:16.008 --> 01:25:16.391
<v LEMELLE>Right.

1051
01:25:16.391 --> 01:25:18.305
<v CRAMER>All that kind of stuff.

1052
01:25:18.305 --> 01:25:25.195
When you and I return we're going to talk a little bit about entertainment, where young folks gathered.

1053
01:25:25.195 --> 01:25:27.109
Ms. Marteel’s Canteen, I think.

1054
01:25:27.109 --> 01:25:29.023
Lula Lyons had a canteen.

1055
01:25:29.023 --> 01:25:32.469
Music, movies, a public library, where that was, school.

1056
01:25:32.469 --> 01:25:34.000
<v LEMELLE>School, school, school.

1057
01:25:34.000 --> 01:25:35.028
<v CRAMER>School, school, school.

1058
01:25:35.028 --> 01:25:38.455
Some of the teachers that were influential in your life.

1059
01:25:38.455 --> 01:25:41.881
I know, for example, Ms. Gloria Rogers was really important.

1060
01:25:41.881 --> 01:25:43.937
Coach Williams, important in the community.

1061
01:25:43.937 --> 01:25:45.308
We'll talk about that.

1062
01:25:45.308 --> 01:25:47.021
We'll talk about medical treatment,

1063
01:25:47.021 --> 01:25:55.245
because wasn't it your dad . . . Didn't your dad . . . Didn't Ms. Julia tell me that your dad had some home remedies and stuff like that?

1064
01:25:55.245 --> 01:25:56.958
I feel like she did.

1065
01:25:56.958 --> 01:25:58.671
I went back to the . . .

1066
01:25:58.671 --> 01:26:02.098
<v LEMELLE>I think my grandmother and them may have had some.

1067
01:26:02.098 --> 01:26:02.441
<v CRAMER>Okay.

1068
01:26:02.441 --> 01:26:06.552
<v LEMELLE>But I can’t remember my daddy having that much of home remedies.

1069
01:26:06.552 --> 01:26:06.895
<v CRAMER>Okay.

1070
01:26:06.895 --> 01:26:08.266
I might be wrong.

1071
01:26:08.266 --> 01:26:09.294
Maybe it was . . .

1072
01:26:09.294 --> 01:26:14.776
<v LEMELLE>Now she might have had something that was done prior to me, because I'm the baby.

1073
01:26:14.776 --> 01:26:16.147
<v CRAMER>You're the baby. [Laughs]

1074
